var e=`Teaching TCG facts for Catch'em Pulse. No invented pull rates or sold prices. 1-in-N figures are TCGPlayer Authentication Center samples, not TPC sheets.`,t=`2026-09-16`,n=296,r=[{id:`tcg-sv-pack-slots`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Scarlet and Violet pack is ten game cards plus energy plus a code.`,stat:`11+code`,sub:`Ten randomized cards. One Basic Energy. One TCG Live code.`,why:`English Scarlet & Violet and Mega Evolution boosters hold ten expansion cards, then a guaranteed Basic Energy, then a Pokémon TCG Live code card. That is eleven playable cards in the wrapper and twelve card-sized pieces. The energy does not replace a hit.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`sv`,`mega-evolution`]},{id:`tcg-wrapper-ten`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The wrapper says ten cards because energy is counted separately.`,stat:`10`,sub:`Energy is extra. The code card is not a playable card.`,why:`Official pack copy still says ten cards. The tenth is the last expansion card, not the energy. Count eleven if you include Basic Energy, twelve if you include the code. Hits replace a foil slot; they do not add an extra card on top.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-sm-swsh-eleven`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Sun and Moon through Sword and Shield packs held eleven cards.`,stat:`11`,sub:`Energy came back as the extra card in 2017.`,why:`Sun & Moon relaunched the eleventh card as a guaranteed Basic Energy, and Sword & Shield kept that count. Diamond & Pearl through XY had dropped to ten cards with no extra energy. The energy is for play, not a chase slot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`sm`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-dp-xy-ten`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Diamond and Pearl through XY packs were ten cards with no extra energy.`,stat:`10`,sub:`If you pulled energy, it took a card slot.`,why:`From Diamond & Pearl in 2007 through XY Evolutions in 2016, an English booster held ten cards. Energy was not a bonus eleventh card. Pulling a Basic Energy meant you got energy instead of another trainer or Pokémon in that slot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`dp`,`xy`]},{id:`tcg-base-eleven`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Original Base Set packs were eleven cards, energy included.`,stat:`1999`,sub:`Wizards counted energy as one of the eleven.`,why:`Wizards of the Coast Base Set boosters in 1999 held eleven cards, and Basic Energy was part of that count. Sun & Moon later brought the extra energy back as a bonus. Two different pack math problems from two different decades.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`vintage`,`base-set`,`wizards`]},{id:`tcg-code-not-play`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A code card is for TCG Live. It is not a twelfth Pokémon.`,stat:`LIVE`,sub:`Redeem it digitally. It has no table rules.`,why:`Every modern English booster tucks a Pokémon TCG Live code behind the cards. It unlocks a digital pack, not a paper card. Throw it only if you do not play Live and do not know someone who does. It never enters a 60-card deck.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`play`,`live`]},{id:`tcg-sv-two-reverse`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Scarlet and Violet packs have two reverse-holo slots.`,stat:`2 REV`,sub:`The second reverse is where illustration rares live.`,why:`Starting with Scarlet & Violet, each pack has two reverse-holo positions after the uncommons. The first is usually a reverse of a common or uncommon. The second can stay a reverse or upgrade into an illustration rare, special illustration rare, ultra rare, or hyper rare.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`sv`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-hit-replaces`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A chase card replaces a slot. It does not add an extra card.`,stat:`SLOT`,sub:`You still leave the pack with the same card count.`,why:`When an illustration rare or special illustration rare shows up, it sits in the second reverse slot. You do not get the reverse and the chase. Same idea for ultra rares and hyper rares. Count the cards; the wrapper never secretly held thirteen.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`sv`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-sv-all-rares-foil`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Every Scarlet and Violet rare is already foil.`,stat:`HOLO`,sub:`There is no non-holo rare in this era.`,why:`Scarlet & Violet dropped non-holofoil rares. The rare slot is always a foil, whether that is a regular rare, a double rare Pokémon ex, or something higher. Reverse holos are a different finish on commons, uncommons, and rares, not a substitute for that guaranteed foil.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`sv`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-sv-commons-uncommons`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A modern pack opens four commons, then three uncommons, then foils.`,stat:`4+3`,sub:`Rarest cards sit at the back of a Scarlet and Violet pack.`,why:`Scarlet & Violet packs are stacked so the first seven cards are bulk: four commons, three uncommons. Foils follow. Energy and the code close the pack. That order is why rip videos flip from the back; the front of the pack is not where the illustration rares hide.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-energy-is-basic`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The energy in a booster is Basic Energy, not ACE SPEC energy.`,stat:`BASIC`,sub:`Eight types. Not a special energy and not a hit.`,why:`The guaranteed energy at the back of a Scarlet & Violet or Mega Evolution pack is one of the eight Basic Energy types: Grass, Fire, Water, Lightning, Psychic, Fighting, Darkness, or Metal. Special Energy and ACE SPEC Energy are randomized hits. Do not price the pack energy as a chase.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`energy`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-jp-five`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Japanese main-set pack is five cards, not ten.`,stat:`5`,sub:`English packs are the wide ones. Do not mix box math.`,why:`Japanese booster packs for regular expansions hold five cards. English packs hold ten expansion cards plus energy. A Japanese box is thirty packs. An English box is thirty-six; per-pack odds, per-box odds, and per-dollar odds are three different questions.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`japanese`,`english`]},{id:`tcg-jp-box-30`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Japanese booster box is thirty packs.`,stat:`30`,sub:`English boxes are thirty-six. Hit guarantees differ too.`,why:`Japanese main-set boxes contain thirty five-card packs. Many Japanese boxes also guarantee at least one super rare or better. English thirty-six-pack boxes do not print that guarantee on the insert. Translate odds before you compare a Japanese box to an English one.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`japanese`,`pack`]},{id:`tcg-en-box-36`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`An English booster box is thirty-six packs.`,stat:`36`,sub:`That number is the box, not the Elite Trainer Box.`,why:`A standard English booster display holds thirty-six packs. Elite Trainer Boxes, booster bundles, and Ultra Premium Collections are different SKUs with different pack counts. Special English sets sometimes skip the thirty-six-pack box entirely.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`english`,`pack`]},{id:`tcg-bundle-six`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A booster bundle is six packs in a small cardboard wrap.`,stat:`6`,sub:`Not a box. Not an Elite Trainer Box.`,why:`The booster bundle is a six-pack retail SKU used across Sword & Shield and Scarlet & Violet. It has no dice, no sleeves, and no promo. Hunt math on a bundle is six packs. Hunt math on a Scarlet & Violet Elite Trainer Box is nine.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`pack`]},{id:`tcg-swsh-etb-8`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Sword and Shield Elite Trainer Box holds eight packs.`,stat:`8`,sub:`Scarlet and Violet Elite Trainer Boxes jumped to nine.`,why:`English Sword & Shield Elite Trainer Boxes packed eight boosters plus sleeves, dice, energy, and a promo. Scarlet & Violet moved the pack count to nine. If you mix eras on a shelf, the ETB is not a constant pack number.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`swsh`,`etb`]},{id:`tcg-sv-etb-9`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Scarlet and Violet Elite Trainer Box holds nine packs.`,stat:`9`,sub:`That is the regular ETB. The Pokémon Center SKU is separate art.`,why:`From Scarlet & Violet base through the later SV specials, the standard English Elite Trainer Box is nine packs. Pokémon Center ETBs use different exterior art and a stamped promo. Count packs on the product panel. Do not assume eight from Sword & Shield memory.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`sv`,`etb`]},{id:`tcg-pc-etb-sku`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Pokémon Center Elite Trainer Box is a different SKU.`,stat:`PC`,sub:`Different art, stamped promo, same set. Price them apart.`,why:`Pokémon Center is The Pokémon Company's own shop. Its Elite Trainer Box is not the mass-retail ETB with a sticker swapped. The promo is stamped, the wrap art is exclusive, and the two boxes trade as separate products. Do not average them.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`etb`]},{id:`tcg-no-pack-art-promise`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Pack art shows Pokémon from the set, not a promised pull.`,stat:`ART`,sub:`The wrapper is marketing. The slots are randomized.`,why:`Official support is blunt: packaging shows featured Pokémon, not the contents of that wrapper. A Charizard on the pack does not mean a Charizard inside. The rare slot and the reverse slots follow the set's table, not the illustration on the foil.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`]},{id:`tcg-reverse-is-finish`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Reverse holo is a finish, not a rarity rank.`,stat:`REV`,sub:`It is a parallel of a common, uncommon, or rare.`,why:`A reverse holofoil is the same card with a foil treatment on the border instead of the art window. Almost every non-secret card in a modern set has a reverse. Pulling a reverse Charizard ex is not the same pull as the special illustration rare Charizard.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-ptcgo-to-live`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Online codes used to redeem in TCG Online. Now they redeem in Live.`,stat:`LIVE`,sub:`Old PTCGO codes do not open a paper pack.`,why:`Sword & Shield packs printed Pokémon TCG Online codes. That client closed. Pokémon TCG Live is the current digital game, and current packs print Live codes. A code is a digital pack with its own drop table; it is not a second paper pack.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`live`]},{id:`tcg-me-pack-same`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Mega Evolution packs keep the Scarlet and Violet slot layout.`,stat:`ME`,sub:`Ten game cards, one energy, one code.`,why:`The Mega Evolution era that started in September 2025 did not rewind pack anatomy. English Mega Evolution boosters still give ten expansion cards, a Basic Energy, and a Live code. What changed is the card pool: Mega Evolution Pokémon ex, not the wrapper math.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`mega-evolution`]},{id:`tcg-ir-what`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`An illustration rare is a full-card painting, usually of a non-ex.`,stat:`IR`,sub:`Gold star. Secret numbering. Not the SIR.`,why:`Illustration rares landed with Scarlet & Violet. The art runs edge to edge, the rarity is a gold star, and the number sits past the set size, like 166/165. Most IRs are ordinary Pokémon, not Pokémon ex. They occupy the second reverse slot when they hit.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`sv`,`ir`]},{id:`tcg-sir-what`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A special illustration rare is the painted chase, usually an ex or a supporter.`,stat:`SIR`,sub:`Same gold star family. Heavier art. Thinner slot.`,why:`Special illustration rares share the secret-rare star with illustration rares, but the paintings are the big set pieces: Pokémon ex, famous supporters, sometimes a legendary in a scene. They land in the same upgrade slot as IRs. Demand is not the same as print.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`sv`,`sir`]},{id:`tcg-ir-vs-sir`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Illustration rare and special illustration rare are different ranks.`,stat:`IR/SIR`,sub:`IR is the full-art scene. SIR is the showcase painting.`,why:`People shorten both to IR, which hides a real ladder. Illustration rares are the wide full-arts. Special illustration rares are the thinner, more painted chases. A set can have dozens of IRs and a handful of SIRs; slot rate for any IR is not the rate for one SIR.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`sv`,`ir`,`sir`]},{id:`tcg-ur-what`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Ultra rare in Scarlet and Violet is the full-art textured ex or trainer.`,stat:`UR`,sub:`Not gold. Not the illustration rare painting.`,why:`Ultra rares are the full-art Pokémon ex and full-art trainers with a textured frame. They are secret rares, numbered past the set size. They are not illustration rares and they are not the gold hyper rares. Three different finishes, three different binders.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`sv`,`ur`]},{id:`tcg-hr-what`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Hyper rare is the gold etched card.`,stat:`HR`,sub:`Gold frame. Usually a Pokémon ex. Secret numbering.`,why:`Hyper rares are the gold cards at the end of a Scarlet & Violet set list. They sit in the same upgrade slot as illustration rares and special illustration rares. Gold is a finish. It is not automatically the hardest pull in the set, and it is not a grade.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`sv`,`hr`]},{id:`tcg-double-rare`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Double rare is the regular Pokémon ex in the main numbered set.`,stat:`RR`,sub:`Two black stars. This is the playable ex, not the SIR.`,why:`Pokémon ex in Scarlet & Violet use the double-rare rarity, marked with two black stars. That card is in the main numbered set and can sit in the rare slot. The special illustration rare of the same Pokémon is a different card with a different number and a different slot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`sv`,`ex`]},{id:`tcg-secret-number`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Secret rares number past the set size.`,stat:`199/165`,sub:`The second number is the main set. The first can run over it.`,why:`If a card reads 199/165, 165 is the last number of the regular set and 199 is a secret. Illustration rares, special illustration rares, ultra rares, and hyper rares all live in that overrun. The fraction is a binder index, not a pull rate.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-ace-spec-one`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`You may play only one ACE SPEC card in a deck.`,stat:`1`,sub:`Powerful trainer or energy. Pink template in Scarlet and Violet.`,why:`ACE SPEC is a deck-building limit, not a flavor text. One copy, in the whole 60. Temporal Forces brought them back in 2024 with a pink frame and a new rarity icon. They can be Items, Tools, Stadiums, or Special Energy; they are not illustration rares.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`play`,`ace-spec`,`temporal-forces`]},{id:`tcg-ace-spec-slot`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`ACE SPEC hits the first reverse slot, not the SIR slot.`,stat:`REV 1`,sub:`You can pull an ACE SPEC and an illustration rare together.`,why:`In Temporal Forces and later Scarlet & Violet sets that print ACE SPEC, that card can replace the first reverse holo. Illustration rares still live in the second reverse. The two slots are independent. ACE SPEC is extra gravy, not a substitute for the special illustration rare.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`pack`,`ace-spec`,`temporal-forces`]},{id:`tcg-ace-spec-bw`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`ACE SPEC first showed up in Black and White Boundaries Crossed.`,stat:`2012`,sub:`Those original thirteen were Item and Tool cards.`,why:`Boundaries Crossed in 2012 printed the first ACE SPEC cards, all Items or Tools, rarity Rare ACE. They ran through Plasma Blast. Scarlet & Violet Temporal Forces revived the rule and added Stadium and Special Energy ACE SPECs. Same one-per-deck law; new pink card.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`ace-spec`,`bw`,`temporal-forces`]},{id:`tcg-rainbow-gone`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Rainbow rares ended when Scarlet and Violet began.`,stat:`SV`,sub:`Sword and Shield still had them. Scarlet and Violet does not.`,why:`Sun & Moon and Sword & Shield printed rainbow secret rares with a spectrum foil. Scarlet & Violet rebuilt the secret-rare ladder around illustration rares, special illustration rares, ultra rares, and hyper rares instead. A rainbow Charizard is an older-era card, not a missing SV slot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`sv`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-shiny-rare`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Shiny rare is a Paldean Fates rank, not a game shiny hunt.`,stat:`SH`,sub:`Kanto and Paldea shinies on a black-gold treatment.`,why:`Paldean Fates printed Shiny rares and Shiny ultra rares as their own rarity, localized from Japanese Shiny Treasure ex. A shiny on the card is a print treatment. It is not the 1-in-4,096 wild shiny from the video games, and it is not a special illustration rare.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`paldean-fates`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-pokeball-parallel`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Poké Ball reverse is a pattern parallel, not a new Pokémon.`,stat:`BALL`,sub:`Same card name. Ball foil on the border.`,why:`Poké Ball patterned cards are reverse-style parallels with a Poké Ball texture in the foil. Japanese high-class packs used them first. English Prismatic Evolutions and Black Bolt / White Flare brought the idea west. It is a finish of an existing card, not a thirteenth rarity icon.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`pokeball`,`prismatic`]},{id:`tcg-masterball-parallel`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Master Ball parallel is the scarcer ball pattern.`,stat:`MASTER`,sub:`Same idea as Poké Ball. Thinner print. Still a parallel.`,why:`Master Ball patterned cards sit above Poké Ball parallels in the same special-set idea: a foil pattern on a regular card, not a new attack box. Japanese 151 high-class packs are famous for them. English Prismatic Evolutions printed Master Ball cards too. Do not call a Master Ball a special illustration rare.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`masterball`,`prismatic`,`151`]},{id:`tcg-151-en-no-masterball`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`English 151 packs do not use Japanese Master Ball reverses.`,stat:`151`,sub:`Those ball patterns are the Japanese high-class printing.`,why:`Japanese Pokémon Card 151 is a high-class pack with Poké Ball and Master Ball patterned reverses. English 151 is a different product: ten-card SV anatomy, illustration rares, special illustration rares. Import a Japanese Master Ball as a Japanese card. Do not expect it from an English 151 Elite Trainer Box.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`151`,`japanese`,`english`,`masterball`]},{id:`tcg-ancient-rule`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Ancient is a rule box on Paradox cards, not an energy type.`,stat:`ANCIENT`,sub:`Past paradox Pokémon. Effects that name other Ancient cards.`,why:`Temporal Forces printed an Ancient label on past-paradox Pokémon such as Walking Wake and Gouging Fire. The word is a mechanic, like a team badge. It is not a ninth Basic Energy. Future is the matching label on iron Pokémon from the other timeline.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`temporal-forces`,`paradox-rift`]},{id:`tcg-future-rule`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Future is the other Paradox rule box, not a rarity.`,stat:`FUTURE`,sub:`Iron Hands, Iron Leaves, Iron Crown. Not a secret rare.`,why:`Future cards are the Miraidon-side paradox Pokémon. Temporal Forces lets Future cards talk to other Future cards, the same way Ancient cards talk to Ancient. The label lives in the text box. Illustration rare and special illustration rare are still the art ranks on top.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`temporal-forces`,`paradox-rift`]},{id:`tcg-tera-type`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Tera Pokémon ex can wear a different type than the species.`,stat:`TERA`,sub:`Crystal body. The type icon is the Tera type.`,why:`Scarlet & Violet Tera Pokémon ex show the Terastal crystal look from the games. The card's type is the Tera type, which can disagree with the Pokédex typing. Weakness follows the card, not the National Dex. Tera is a treatment of an ex, not a pull-rate rank.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`sv`,`ex`]},{id:`tcg-mega-three-prizes`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Mega Evolution Pokémon ex are worth three prize cards.`,stat:`3`,sub:`Regular Pokémon ex are still two prizes.`,why:`When the Mega Evolution era opened in 2025, Mega Evolution Pokémon ex gave up three prizes on knockout. Ordinary Pokémon ex still give two. That is a play rule printed on the card, not a rarity. Building around a Mega means you are playing a three-prize attacker.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`mega-evolution`,`ex`]},{id:`tcg-ex-two-prizes`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Knocking out a Pokémon ex takes two prize cards.`,stat:`2`,sub:`That rule is older than Scarlet and Violet.`,why:`Pokémon-EX, Pokémon-GX, Pokémon V, and Pokémon ex all give two prizes when they hit the discard. Mega Evolution ex later moved to three. Prize count is how the rules tax powerful attackers. It is not how many packs the card came from.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`ex`]},{id:`tcg-radiant-one`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Radiant Pokémon are a one-per-deck Sword and Shield rule.`,stat:`R`,sub:`Not an illustration rare. Not a shiny from Paldean Fates.`,why:`Radiant Pokémon, introduced in Sword & Shield, carry a one-per-deck restriction and a radiant frame. They are main-set cards, not secret illustration rares. Paldean Fates shiny rares are a later, different treatment. Do not mix the words radiant, shiny, and illustration.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`swsh`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-prism-star`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Prism Star cards were a one-per-deck Sun and Moon rule.`,stat:`☆`,sub:`Ultra Prism through the rest of Sun and Moon.`,why:`Prism Star cards, marked with a star in the name, allowed only one copy in the deck and could not be copied by some search effects. They are a Sun & Moon mechanic. They are not Scarlet & Violet illustration rares, even though both use star language in casual talk.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`sm`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-en-1999`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`English Pokémon TCG landed in 1999 under Wizards of the Coast.`,stat:`1999`,sub:`Japan had already been playing since 1996.`,why:`Wizards of the Coast brought Base Set to English-language stores in 1999, three years after the Japanese launch. The cards used English text, a yellow border, and Wizards' print runs. The Pokémon Company later took the English license back. Same game, different publisher.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`english`,`base-set`]},{id:`tcg-jp-1996`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The Japanese TCG launched in 1996 with the games.`,stat:`1996`,sub:`English Base Set is not the first printing of these cards.`,why:`Media Factory released the Japanese Pokémon Trading Card Game in 1996, the same year as Pocket Monsters Red and Green. English collectors often treat 1999 Base Set as year zero. Japanese first printings are older paper. Language is not the start of the game.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`japanese`]},{id:`tcg-wotc-to-tpc`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The Pokémon Company took English printing in 2003.`,stat:`2003`,sub:`EX Ruby and Sapphire is the first TPC English era set.`,why:`Wizards of the Coast printed English cards from Base Set through the e-Card sets. When the license moved, EX Ruby & Sapphire opened the EX era under Nintendo and The Pokémon Company. First Edition stamps stopped with that handoff. A 2004 card cannot be a Wizards 1st Edition.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`ex-era`]},{id:`tcg-1st-ed-stamp`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`First Edition is a stamp on the card, not a grade.`,stat:`1ST`,sub:`Look left of the artwork. PSA still grades the cardboard.`,why:`Wizards marked early English print runs with a small 'Edition 1' stamp beside the art window. Unlimited is the later print without that stamp. A First Edition 10 is a stamp plus a grade. The stamp does not make the corners mint by itself.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`grade`,`base-set`]},{id:`tcg-unlimited-later`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Unlimited is the later Wizards print without the Edition 1 stamp.`,stat:`UNL`,sub:`Same set symbol. Same attacks. Later, larger run.`,why:`After the First Edition window, Wizards kept printing the set as Unlimited. The attacks did not change. The stamp came off, and Base Set Unlimited also picked up a drop shadow on the art box. Unlimited is a print, not a condition; near mint Unlimited is still near mint.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`base-set`]},{id:`tcg-shadowless-print`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Shadowless is a Base Set print run, not a condition.`,stat:`SHDW`,sub:`No drop shadow on the right edge of the art box.`,why:`Early Unlimited Base Set, plus every First Edition Base card, lacks the drop shadow that later Unlimited added to the illustration window. Collectors call that short Unlimited run Shadowless. It is a factory layout, not a PSA qualifier and not a missing ink error.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`base-set`]},{id:`tcg-1st-ed-is-shadowless`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Every First Edition Base Set card is also shadowless.`,stat:`1ST`,sub:`The stamp is how you tell it from Shadowless Unlimited.`,why:`First Edition Base Set used the early layout: thin HP font, no drop shadow. Shadowless Unlimited used that same layout without the stamp. If you only check for a shadow, you will mix the two. Check the left side of the art for Edition 1.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`base-set`]},{id:`tcg-shadow-tells-unlimited`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A drop shadow on a Base Set art box means Unlimited.`,stat:`UNL`,sub:`Shadow present, no stamp. That is the mass print.`,why:`Later Base Set Unlimited added a dark drop shadow along the right of the illustration window and thickened the HP numbers. That is the card most people picture as 'Base Set.' It is not First Edition and it is not the short Shadowless Unlimited run.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`base-set`]},{id:`tcg-base-102`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`English Base Set is 102 cards, energy included.`,stat:`102`,sub:`Sixteen holos. Charizard is 4 of 102, not dex 6.`,why:`Wizards Base Set numbered 1 through 102, with holos in the low numbers and Basic Energy at the end. National Dex Charizard is 006, but Base Set Charizard is 4/102. Binder numbers and Pokédex numbers are two lists.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`base-set`,`charizard`],cardId:`base1-4`},{id:`tcg-base-zard-4`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The first English Charizard is Base Set 4 of 102.`,stat:`4/102`,sub:`Mitsuhiro Arita art. Wizards, 1999.`,why:`English Base Set Charizard is card 4/102, illustrated by Mitsuhiro Arita. The 151 special illustration rare Charizard is a 2023 Scarlet & Violet card of the same monster. Same species, different cardboard, different era, different slot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`base-set`,`charizard`],cardId:`base1-4`},{id:`tcg-arita-zard`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Mitsuhiro Arita drew the Base Set Charizard.`,stat:`ARITA`,sub:`The artist credit is printed on the card.`,why:`Arita's Charizard is the image people mean by 'Base Set Charizard.' Later Charizard cards use other illustrators and other frames. When someone says they pulled a Charizard, ask which set and which rarity. The species is not the printing.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`base-set`,`charizard`],cardId:`base1-4`},{id:`tcg-jungle-second`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Jungle was the second English expansion.`,stat:`64`,sub:`Wizards, 1999. Sixty-four cards after Base Set.`,why:`Jungle followed Base Set in 1999 with 64 cards and the first English look at Pokémon such as Scyther, Flareon, and Vaporeon as holos. It had First Edition and Unlimited prints. Jungle is a set name, not a type, and not a Jungle-themed modern secret rare.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`jungle`]},{id:`tcg-fossil-third`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Fossil was the third English expansion.`,stat:`62`,sub:`Aerodactyl, Articuno, and the first English Lapras holo.`,why:`Fossil arrived in 1999 with 62 cards. It is a Wizards set with First Edition and Unlimited, not a fossil-mechanic from later games. Aerodactyl, the legendary birds as holos, and Dragonite live here. Three early sets: Base, Jungle, Fossil.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`fossil`]},{id:`tcg-team-rocket-dark`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Team Rocket Dark Pokémon were names, not Darkness type.`,stat:`TR`,sub:`Dark Charizard is still a Fire card in that set.`,why:`Team Rocket in 2000 printed Dark Charizard, Dark Blastoise, and friends. Dark was a Team Rocket affiliation in the name. Darkness as an energy type arrived later in Neo. A Team Rocket Dark Pokémon is not a Darkness-type card and not a Destined Rivals Team Rocket's Pokémon.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`team-rocket`,`charizard`]},{id:`tcg-gym-owners`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Gym Heroes and Gym Challenge printed owner Pokémon first.`,stat:`GYM`,sub:`Blaine's, Misty's, Rocket's. That is a trainer's Pokémon.`,why:`Wizards Gym Heroes and Gym Challenge in 2000 put gym leaders' names on the Pokémon. That is the ancestor of Journey Together's N's and Iono's cards and of Destined Rivals' Team Rocket's Pokémon. Owner text is a game line, not a grade.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`gym`,`journey-together`]},{id:`tcg-neo-johto`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Neo Genesis brought Johto, Darkness, and Metal to English.`,stat:`2000`,sub:`Umbreon and Espeon enter the English TCG here.`,why:`Neo Genesis opened the Neo era in 2000. It added Johto Pokémon and introduced Darkness and Metal as types with their own Basic Energy. Umbreon and Espeon are Neo cards first. A modern Umbreon special illustration rare is not a Neo pull.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`neo`,`umbreon`,`espeon`]},{id:`tcg-neo-four`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Neo ran four English sets: Genesis, Discovery, Revelation, Destiny.`,stat:`NEO`,sub:`Shining Pokémon hide in Revelation and Destiny.`,why:`Neo Genesis, Neo Discovery, Neo Revelation, and Neo Destiny are the Johto-era Wizards block. Shining Magikarp and other Shining Pokémon are secret-style cards from the later Neo sets. Shining in 2001 is not Paldean Fates shiny rare and not a game-shiny odds table.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`neo`]},{id:`tcg-ecard-ereader`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The e-Card sets carry an e-Reader stripe on the right edge.`,stat:`e`,sub:`Expedition, Aquapolis, Skyridge. Last Wizards block.`,why:`Expedition Base Set, Aquapolis, and Skyridge printed a dot-code strip for Nintendo's e-Reader. Those sets closed the Wizards English license. Crystal-type cards in Aquapolis and Skyridge used a transparent crystal treatment. The stripe is how you identify the era at a glance.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`ecard`,`wizards`]},{id:`tcg-crystal-type`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Crystal-type cards are an e-Card transparent treatment.`,stat:`CRYSTAL`,sub:`Aquapolis and Skyridge. Not a Scarlet and Violet Tera.`,why:`Crystal Pokémon in Aquapolis and Skyridge show a see-through crystal body and a special rarity. They needed the e-Reader era layout. Tera Pokémon ex in Scarlet & Violet are a different crystal idea from the video games, twenty-plus years later. Do not file them together.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`ecard`]},{id:`tcg-1st-ed-ended`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`English First Edition stopped when Wizards left.`,stat:`2003`,sub:`EX era cards have no Edition 1 stamp.`,why:`First Edition English stamps exist on Wizards sets from Base through the e-Card block, with messy timing on the last few. The Pokémon Company did not continue that stamp on EX Ruby & Sapphire. A modern 151 card cannot be First Edition. Anyone listing one that way is mixing terms.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`ex-era`]},{id:`tcg-base-set-2`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Base Set 2 is a reprint set, not a sequel story.`,stat:`BS2`,sub:`Wizards reprinted Base and Jungle cards with a new symbol.`,why:`Base Set 2 took cards from Base Set and Jungle, gave them a new set symbol, and printed another English run. It is why some early cards exist in extra printings. A Base Set 2 Charizard is not Base Set 4/102. Check the symbol in the corner.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`,`base-set`,`charizard`]},{id:`tcg-legendary-collection`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Legendary Collection reprinted Wizards holos with a reverse fire.`,stat:`LC`,sub:`Reverse holos as a collectible finish start becoming normal here.`,why:`Legendary Collection in 2002 reprinted many early cards and is widely remembered for its reverse-holo treatment. Reverse holos later became a standard pack slot. A Legendary Collection reverse is a 2002 reprint, not a First Edition Base card.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`]},{id:`tcg-error-not-rarity`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`An error card is a print mistake, not a secret rare.`,stat:`ERR`,sub:`Miscuts, crimps, missing ink. Factory, not a slot.`,why:`Crimps, off-center cuts, ink wipe, and wrong backs happen at the plant. Some collectors hunt them. They do not have a set-list number or a pull table. A miscut Charizard is still a Charizard from that set; do not invent a 1-in-N for a factory accident.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`error`]},{id:`tcg-alignment-dot`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Alignment dots are printer marks, not a hidden set symbol.`,stat:`DOT`,sub:`You see them on badly centered cuts.`,why:`Sheets include registration marks so the cutter can line up. When a card is miscut far enough, those dots show on the face. That is evidence of a cut, not a promotional stamp and not a First Edition mark. Centered cards hide the dots because they were cut correctly.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`error`]},{id:`tcg-copyright-line`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Base Set copyright lines help date Unlimited prints.`,stat:`©`,sub:`Early cards say 1999. Later Unlimited adds 2000.`,why:`First Edition and Shadowless Base Set use a 1999 copyright. Later Unlimited prints add 2000 to the copyright line and keep the drop shadow. The line is a print clue. It is not a grade and it is not a 1st Edition stamp.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`base-set`,`wizards`]},{id:`tcg-yellow-border`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`English vintage cards wear a yellow border. Japanese cards do not.`,stat:`YELLOW`,sub:`The back is still the familiar Pokémon back.`,why:`Wizards English cards framed the face in yellow. Japanese cards of the same era use a different face frame without that English yellow ring. The reverse Pokémon back is shared. Language and border tell you which printing you are holding before you read a single attack.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`english`,`japanese`]},{id:`tcg-southern-islands`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Southern Islands is a promo boxed set, not a booster expansion.`,stat:`SI`,sub:`Eighteen cards. No Jungle-style booster box.`,why:`Southern Islands was a 2001 Wizards collection with its own art, sold as a boxed promo set rather than a thirty-six-pack display. It is easy to file next to Jungle because of the theme. It is not the second expansion, and it has no First Edition booster-stamp story like Base Set.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`wizards`]},{id:`tcg-ex-era`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The EX era opened with Ruby and Sapphire in 2003.`,stat:`EX`,sub:`Pokémon-EX give two prizes. Gold Stars hide in later EX sets.`,why:`EX Ruby & Sapphire is the first English set after Wizards. Pokémon-EX, written in caps, are the two-prize attackers of that era. Gold Star secret rares appear later in the EX block. An EX-era Pokémon-EX is not a Scarlet & Violet Pokémon ex, even if the letters match in speech.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`ex-era`]},{id:`tcg-ex-vs-ex`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Pokémon-EX and Pokémon ex are different printings from different eras.`,stat:`EX/ex`,sub:`Caps EX is 2000s. Lowercase ex starts in 2023.`,why:`Black & White and XY reprinted the Pokémon-EX idea with a red EX mark. Scarlet & Violet restarted it as Pokémon ex with a lowercase tag and a new card frame. They both give two prizes. They are not legal as the same card, and they do not share a pull table.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`ex-era`,`sv`,`xy`,`bw`]},{id:`tcg-gold-star`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Gold Star Pokémon are an EX-era secret rare.`,stat:`★`,sub:`Shiny look, gold star at the name, very thin print.`,why:`Gold Stars, from sets such as Hidden Legends through Power Keepers, show a shiny Pokémon with a star next to the name. They are among the scarcest EX-era pulls. A Gold Star is not a Scarlet & Violet illustration rare, even though both casual nicknames use the word star.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`ex-era`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-dp-lvx`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Diamond and Pearl added Pokémon LV.X as the extra stage.`,stat:`LV.X`,sub:`A Level-Up card, not an evolution in the Pokédex sense.`,why:`Diamond & Pearl in 2007 printed LV.X cards that sit on top of a Pokémon in play and add attacks. English packs shrank to ten cards in this era. LV.X is not VMAX, not VSTAR, and not a Mega Evolution. Each of those is a later, separate extra-stage idea.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`dp`]},{id:`tcg-platinum-sp`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Platinum printed SP Pokémon with trainer faces in the art.`,stat:`SP`,sub:`Cynthia, Team Galactic, and other Sinnoh trainers.`,why:`Platinum-era SP cards are Pokémon paired with a trainer, a mid-2000s cousin of Gym Heroes and of Journey Together. Rising Rivals, Supreme Victors, and Arceus close that block. An SP Cynthia's Garchomp is not Destined Rivals Cynthia's Garchomp.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`dp`,`platinum`]},{id:`tcg-hgss-2010`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`HeartGold SoulSilver opened in English in 2010.`,stat:`HGSS`,sub:`Johto again. LEGEND cards take two slots in the deck.`,why:`HeartGold & SoulSilver brought Johto back and printed two-card LEGEND Pokémon that occupy two deck slots and two bench spaces. Call of Legends closed the block in 2011. HGSS is an era name. HeartGold the Game Boy game is not a pull rate.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`hgss`]},{id:`tcg-legend-two`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`LEGEND Pokémon are two cards that count as two in the deck.`,stat:`LEGEND`,sub:`They play as one Pokémon once both halves are in play.`,why:`HGSS LEGEND cards split a legendary across two pieces of cardboard. Both halves must be in the deck, and both enter play together. That is a 2010 mechanic. It is not a tag team, not a VUNION, and not a Twin card from later sets.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`hgss`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-bw-2011`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Black and White relaunched Pokémon-EX in 2011.`,stat:`BW`,sub:`Full-art EX and later ACE SPEC live in this era.`,why:`Black & White base set arrived in April 2011. The block brought Pokémon-EX back with full-art versions, Team Plasma story sets, and the original ACE SPEC cards. English packs were still ten cards. Fairy type had not been invented yet.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`bw`]},{id:`tcg-xy-fairy`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`XY introduced Fairy type to the TCG in 2014.`,stat:`FAIRY`,sub:`Sword and Shield later removed Fairy as a type.`,why:`When X and Y landed in the games, the TCG added Fairy energy and Fairy Pokémon. Sylveon is the poster Fairy. Sword & Shield in 2020 dropped Fairy as a type and folded those Pokémon into Psychic. A Fairy Energy from XY is a legal energy only in formats that still allow that printing's rules.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`xy`,`energy`]},{id:`tcg-xy-mega-first`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`XY printed the first Mega Evolution cards in 2014.`,stat:`M`,sub:`Those Megas are not the 2025 Mega Evolution ex cards.`,why:`XY Mega Evolutions were a one-turn evolution on a Pokémon-EX, often with a Dragon-type Mega and a two-prize cost. The 2025 Mega Evolution era reprints the idea with new frames, three-prize Mega ex, and a new regulation mark. Same monster form. New cardboard.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`xy`,`mega-evolution`]},{id:`tcg-xy-evolutions`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`XY Evolutions reprinted Base Set art for the TCG's 20th year.`,stat:`EVO`,sub:`2016. It is a reprint set, not 1999 cardboard.`,why:`Evolutions, the last XY set, redrew Base Set in the XY template. The Charizard looks familiar on purpose. It is a 2016 card with an XY set symbol. It is not Wizards Base Set 4/102, not First Edition, and not shadowless.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`xy`,`base-set`,`charizard`]},{id:`tcg-sm-gx`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Sun and Moon's big attackers are Pokémon-GX.`,stat:`GX`,sub:`A GX attack once per game. Two prizes on knockout.`,why:`Sun & Moon replaced EX with GX in 2017. Each GX has a once-per-game GX attack. Tag Team GX later put two Pokémon on one card and gave three prizes. A GX is not a V and not an ex; check the mark in the name before you sleeve a Standard pile.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sm`]},{id:`tcg-tag-team`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Tag Team GX cards put two Pokémon on one card and take three prizes.`,stat:`TAG`,sub:`Cosmic Eclipse closed Sun and Moon with many of them.`,why:`Tag Team GX, from Team Up through Cosmic Eclipse, pair two Pokémon and usually give three prize cards when knocked out. That three-prize tax is the same idea Mega Evolution ex uses in 2025, on a different frame. Two eras, one play lesson: bigger body, more prizes.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sm`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-hidden-fates`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Hidden Fates is a Sun and Moon special set with a Shiny Vault.`,stat:`HIF`,sub:`No regular English thirty-six-pack box. Shinies in the vault.`,why:`Hidden Fates in 2019 was a special expansion: ETBs, tins, and collections, not a standard booster-box release. The Shiny Vault numbered SV cards are the chase shinies. Shining Fates later repeated the idea in Sword & Shield. Special set means special SKUs.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sm`,`sealed`,`hidden-fates`]},{id:`tcg-swsh-v`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Sword and Shield replaced GX with Pokémon V.`,stat:`V`,sub:`VMAX and VSTAR are extra stages on top of V.`,why:`Sword & Shield base set launched in February 2020 with Pokémon V as the two-prize attacker. VMAX is a later evolution of a V. VSTAR, from Brilliant Stars onward, is another extra stage with a once-per-game VSTAR power. Moonbreon is a VMAX, not a V and not an ex.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-vmax`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`VMAX is the Dynamax-sized extra stage on a Pokémon V.`,stat:`VMAX`,sub:`Evolving Skies alt-art VMAX are the era's famous chases.`,why:`A VMAX evolves from its V and stays a two-prize Pokémon with a bigger HP box. Alternate-art VMAX in Evolving Skies, including Umbreon VMAX, sit in a thin secret slot. The Dynamax look is flavor. The slot is the collectible.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`,`evolving-skies`]},{id:`tcg-vstar`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`VSTAR is a Sword and Shield extra stage with a once-per-game power.`,stat:`VSTAR`,sub:`Brilliant Stars introduced it. Crown Zenith still prints them.`,why:`VSTAR cards evolve from a V and carry a VSTAR Power you may use once per game. They are not VMAX. Lost Origin Giratina VSTAR and Crown Zenith gold VSTAR cards are this family. A VSTAR special art is a Sword & Shield secret, not a Scarlet & Violet SIR.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-trainer-gallery`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Trainer Gallery is a Sword and Shield subset with TG numbers.`,stat:`TG`,sub:`Brilliant Stars started it. Same pack, extra numbering.`,why:`From Brilliant Stars through Silver Tempest, English sets included a Trainer Gallery: full-art cards numbered TG01 and up. They share the parent set's TCG Live code. A TG card is not a Galarian Gallery card from Crown Zenith. Different subset, different list.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-sv-lowercase-ex`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Scarlet and Violet restarted the two-prize attacker as Pokémon ex.`,stat:`ex`,sub:`March 31, 2023. New frame, new regulation G.`,why:`Scarlet & Violet base set launched the lowercase-ex era, illustration rares, and the two-reverse pack. Regulation mark G begins here. Later SV sets keep the ex frame even after G rotates. The monster on the card can be Paldean, Kanto, or paradox; the frame is the era.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-fairy-left`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Sword and Shield removed Fairy as a TCG type.`,stat:`2020`,sub:`Sylveon became Psychic. Fairy Energy left the basic list.`,why:`When Sword & Shield launched, Fairy Energy stopped being a Basic Energy and Fairy Pokémon were retyped, usually to Psychic. XY through Sun & Moon decks that leaned on Fairy did not carry forward. Scarlet & Violet still has no Fairy Basic Energy. The type lived six years.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`,`xy`,`energy`]},{id:`tcg-reg-marks-start`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Regulation marks begin in Sword and Shield.`,stat:`D`,sub:`The letter is near the artist name, not in the set symbol.`,why:`Play! Pokémon prints a small letter on modern cards so Standard can rotate by letter instead of by set name. Sword & Shield opened with D. Later letters followed. Vintage Wizards cards have no regulation mark; their legality in older formats is a different document.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`swsh`,`rotation`]},{id:`tcg-151-name`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The set named 151 is a Kanto Pokédex joke from 2023.`,stat:`MEW`,sub:`One hundred fifty-one species. Mew is number 151.`,why:`Scarlet & Violet—151 reprints the original Kanto list as modern cards. The English set code people type is MEW. Mew is National Dex 151 and the punchline. These are 2023 printings, not Wizards reprints, and not First Edition Base Set.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`151`,`kanto`,`mew`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-151-no-box`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`English 151 never got a thirty-six-pack booster box.`,stat:`NO BB`,sub:`Hunt it from ETBs, bundles, collections, and the UPC.`,why:`The Pokémon Company treated 151 as a special expansion. There is no English 36-pack display. The Ultra Premium Collection at sixteen packs is the largest official pack SKU. Do not compare a 151 'box' video from another language to English sealed math.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`151`,`sealed`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-151-upc-16`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The 151 Ultra Premium Collection holds sixteen packs.`,stat:`16`,sub:`Largest English 151 pack SKU. Still not a booster box.`,why:`The English 151 Ultra Premium Collection includes sixteen 151 boosters plus accessories and exclusive promos. An Elite Trainer Box is nine packs. A bundle is six. Sixteen is the ceiling TPC put on a single 151 SKU; that is a product fact, not a print-stop date.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`151`,`sealed`,`upc`]},{id:`tcg-151-etb-9`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A 151 Elite Trainer Box still holds nine packs.`,stat:`9`,sub:`Special set, same Scarlet and Violet ETB pack count.`,why:`151 is special because it skipped the booster box, not because the ETB changed size. The English 151 Elite Trainer Box is nine packs, in line with other Scarlet & Violet ETBs. Nine times four is thirty-six, which is why people joke that four ETBs are a box.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`151`,`sealed`,`etb`]},{id:`tcg-151-zard-225`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The 151 Charizard special illustration rare is about one in 225 packs.`,stat:`1/225`,sub:`TCGPlayer Authentication Center. Thicker than Moonbreon.`,why:`English 151 Charizard ex SIR landed about once in 225 packs in TCGPlayer's counted sample. The any-SIR slot in that set was near one in 32, with seven SIRs sharing it. Moonbreon is about one in 2,000. Chase is not one number.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`151`,`charizard`,`sir`],cardId:`sv3pt5-199`},{id:`tcg-151-sir-slot`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Early Scarlet and Violet sets put any special illustration rare near one in 32 packs.`,stat:`1/32`,sub:`That is the slot. A specific SIR is thinner.`,why:`TCGPlayer's Authentication Center counts on Scarlet & Violet base, Paldea Evolved, Obsidian Flames, and 151 show the SIR slot around one in 32 packs. Divide by how many SIRs share the slot to get a specific card. Later SV main sets thinned that slot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sv`,`sir`,`151`,`obsidian-flames`]},{id:`tcg-151-jp-first`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Japanese 151 released in June 2023. English followed in September.`,stat:`JP FIRST`,sub:`Three months. High-class Japanese packs are not English packs.`,why:`Pokémon Card 151 went on sale in Japan on June 16, 2023. English 151 arrived September 22, 2023. Japanese 151 is a high-class 10-card pack with ball-pattern reverses. English 151 uses Scarlet & Violet pack anatomy; same Pokédex joke, different product.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`151`,`japanese`,`english`]},{id:`tcg-151-demigod`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A 151 demigod pack lines up one Kanto starter family as secrets.`,stat:`LINE`,sub:`Bulbasaur, Charmander, or Squirtle lines can fill the foil slots.`,why:`English 151 has a known pack pattern where the Charmander, Squirtle, or Bulbasaur evolutionary line can appear together as secret cards in one wrapper. Collectors call that a demigod pack. It is a printed pattern, not a promised hit on every Elite Trainer Box.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`151`,`pack`,`charizard`]},{id:`tcg-prismatic-no-box`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`English Prismatic Evolutions has no thirty-six-pack booster box.`,stat:`NO BB`,sub:`The Elite Trainer Box is nine packs. That is the sealed unit.`,why:`Prismatic Evolutions, like 151, skipped the English booster display. Hunt math uses Elite Trainer Boxes, bundles, and other collections. Anyone quoting a 36-pack Prismatic box is mixing in a product TPC did not ship in English.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`prismatic`,`sealed`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-prismatic-eevee`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Prismatic Evolutions is the Eeveelution special set.`,stat:`PRE`,sub:`January 17, 2025. Special illustration rares for the Eevees.`,why:`Prismatic Evolutions is a Scarlet & Violet special expansion built around Eevee and the eight evolutions. Sunbreon is the nickname for the Umbreon ex special illustration rare. Poké Ball and Master Ball patterned cards are part of this English set, unlike English 151.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`prismatic`,`eevee`,`umbreon`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-sunbreon-1440`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Sunbreon is about one in 1,440 English Prismatic packs.`,stat:`1/1440`,sub:`TCGPlayer Authentication Center. No English 36-pack box.`,why:`The Umbreon ex special illustration rare from Prismatic Evolutions counted near one in 1,440 packs in TCGPlayer's sample. That is thinner than the 151 Charizard SIR and thicker than Evolving Skies Moonbreon. Prismatic never printed a 36-pack English booster box. English still has ETBs, bundles, and surprise boxes.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`prismatic`,`umbreon`,`sir`],cardId:`sv8pt5-161`},{id:`tcg-prismatic-balls`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Prismatic Evolutions printed Poké Ball and Master Ball cards in English.`,stat:`BALL`,sub:`That parallel layer is why the set feels like a Japanese high-class pack.`,why:`English Prismatic Evolutions copied the Japanese high-class idea of Poké Ball and Master Ball patterned cards on top of illustration rares and special illustration rares. English 151 did not. Two Kanto-adjacent special sets, two different extra layers.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`prismatic`,`pokeball`,`masterball`]},{id:`tcg-surging-sparks`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Surging Sparks is a November 2024 main set with a deep secret pool.`,stat:`SSP`,sub:`Pikachu ex is the famous chase. Thirty-six-pack box exists.`,why:`Surging Sparks is a regular Scarlet & Violet expansion, not a special. It has an English booster box. TCGPlayer counted any special illustration rare near one in 87 packs, in line with later SV mains. The secret-rare list is long, so a specific SIR is thinner than the slot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`surging-sparks`,`pikachu`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-later-sir-87`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Later Scarlet and Violet main sets thinned the SIR slot to about one in 87.`,stat:`1/87`,sub:`Temporal Forces onward. Early SV was near one in 32.`,why:`TCGPlayer's Authentication Center shows Temporal Forces, Twilight Masquerade, Surging Sparks, and Journey Together landing any special illustration rare around one in 86 to 90 packs. Early SV sat near one in 32. The slot changed. A 2023 SIR rate is the wrong table for a 2025 main set.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sv`,`sir`,`temporal-forces`,`surging-sparks`]},{id:`tcg-obsidian-flames`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Obsidian Flames is the Charizard Tera set from August 2023.`,stat:`OBF`,sub:`Main set. English booster box. Early SIR slot.`,why:`Obsidian Flames is the third Scarlet & Violet main expansion. Tera Charizard ex is the mascot. It has a 36-pack English box. The SIR slot still sat near one in 32 packs, with six SIRs sharing it; that is the early-SV table, not the later one-in-87 table.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`obsidian-flames`,`charizard`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-obsidian-sir-192`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A specific Obsidian Flames special illustration rare is about one in 192 packs.`,stat:`1/192`,sub:`TCGPlayer counted the Charizard SIR in that class.`,why:`Obsidian Flames had six special illustration rares sharing a slot TCGPlayer measured near one in 32. A specific SIR, including Charizard ex, counted about one in 192 English packs. That is thicker than Moonbreon and in the same neighborhood as the 151 Charizard SIR.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`obsidian-flames`,`charizard`,`sir`],cardId:`sv3-223`},{id:`tcg-paldean-fates`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Paldean Fates is the shiny special set, not a main expansion.`,stat:`PAF`,sub:`January 26, 2024. No English thirty-six-pack box.`,why:`Paldean Fates localizes Japanese Shiny Treasure ex plus leftover Paradox cards. It is a special SKU lineup: ETBs, collections, tins. No English booster box. Shiny rares are the volume chase; Special illustration rares in this set are a thinner slot than early SV mains.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`paldean-fates`,`sv`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-paf-shiny-four`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Paldean Fates lands some shiny rare in about one in four packs.`,stat:`1/4`,sub:`TCGPlayer Authentication Center. That is any shiny, not a specific one.`,why:`TCGPlayer counted a Shiny rare in roughly one of every four English Paldean Fates packs. That is the any-shiny slot, which is why the set feels showered in shinies. A specific shiny, or a shiny ultra rare, is not one in four. Slot versus card is the whole lesson.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`paldean-fates`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-twilight-masquerade`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Twilight Masquerade is the Teal Mask Ogerpon and Greninja set.`,stat:`TWM`,sub:`May 24, 2024. Main set with a 36-pack box.`,why:`Twilight Masquerade is a regular Scarlet & Violet expansion: Kitakami, Ogerpon, Bloodmoon Ursaluna, and a widely chased Greninja ex special illustration rare. TCGPlayer put any SIR near one in 86 packs. It is an H-mark set, not the G-mark year.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`twilight-masquerade`,`greninja`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-stellar-crown`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Stellar Crown is the Terapagos and Area Zero set.`,stat:`SCR`,sub:`September 13, 2024. Main set. Stellar Tera theme.`,why:`Stellar Crown focuses on Terapagos and the Area Zero crystal look. It is a 36-pack main set. TCGPlayer's later comparison table put its any-SIR rate near one in 90 packs, in the thinned later-SV band. Terapagos is the mascot, not a regulation-mark name.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`stellar-crown`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-destined-rivals`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Destined Rivals puts Team Rocket and famous trainers on the Pokémon.`,stat:`DRI`,sub:`May 30, 2025. Main set. Two hundred forty-four cards.`,why:`Destined Rivals is a regular Scarlet & Violet expansion, so it has an English booster box. Team Rocket's Mewtwo, Cynthia's Garchomp, and Ethan's Ho-Oh are the poster trainer Pokémon. It is not Black Bolt, and it is not the 2000 Team Rocket Dark set.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`destined-rivals`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-journey-together`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Journey Together brought Trainer's Pokémon back to a main set.`,stat:`JTG`,sub:`March 28, 2025. N, Iono, Lillie, and Hop.`,why:`Journey Together is the ninth Scarlet & Violet main expansion. It prints Pokémon owned by N, Iono, Lillie, and Hop, the first main-set trainer Pokémon wave since EX Team Magma vs Team Aqua. TCGPlayer counted any SIR near one in 86 packs, in the later-SV band.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`journey-together`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-trainers-pokemon-return`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Trainer's Pokémon were missing from main sets for years.`,stat:`OWNER`,sub:`Gym Heroes had them. Journey Together brought them back.`,why:`Owner Pokémon — Misty's, N's, Team Rocket's — are a TCG tradition, not a new 2025 gimmick. They vanished from main English expansions for a long stretch after the EX Team Magma vs Team Aqua era. Journey Together and Destined Rivals put the owner line back in the name box.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`journey-together`,`destined-rivals`,`gym`]},{id:`tcg-black-bolt-white-flare`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Black Bolt and White Flare are the Unova dual sets.`,stat:`BLK/WHT`,sub:`July 18, 2025. First English split like Japan.`,why:`Black Bolt and White Flare released together as the last Scarlet & Violet special expansion, covering all 156 Unova Pokémon. English finally got a dual-set release the way Japan does. Zekrom headlines Black Bolt. Reshiram headlines White Flare; they are two set lists, not one box with two names.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`black-bolt`,`white-flare`,`unova`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-bb-wf-no-box`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Black Bolt and White Flare have no English booster boxes.`,stat:`NO BB`,sub:`Special sets. Packs live in ETBs, bundles, and collections.`,why:`Because they are special expansions, TPC did not sell English 36-pack displays of Black Bolt or White Flare. Elite Trainer Boxes, binder collections, and later booster bundles are the pack sources. Dual set does not mean dual boxes.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`black-bolt`,`white-flare`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-bb-wf-pokeball`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Black Bolt and White Flare print Poké Ball pattern parallels.`,stat:`BALL`,sub:`Commons through rares can wear the ball foil.`,why:`The Unova dual sets print regular, Poké Ball, and scarcer Master Ball-style parallels on the creature cards. That is a special-set collecting layer on top of illustration rares. Completing 'every Unova Pokémon' and completing 'every parallel' are different binders.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`black-bolt`,`white-flare`,`pokeball`]},{id:`tcg-evolving-skies`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Evolving Skies is the Sword and Shield Eeveelution set.`,stat:`EVS`,sub:`August 27, 2021. Thirty-six-pack box. Alt-art VMAX.`,why:`Evolving Skies is a main Sword & Shield expansion with a 36-pack English box. Alternate-art Eeveelution V and VMAX cards, plus Rayquaza, are the chases. Moonbreon is the nickname for Umbreon VMAX alt. It is not Prismatic Evolutions and not Neo Genesis.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`evolving-skies`,`umbreon`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-moonbreon-2000`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Moonbreon is 1 in 1,994 Evolving Skies packs`,stat:`1/1994`,sub:`TCGPlayer table. Same pull as Rayquaza VMAX alt.`,why:`Umbreon VMAX alternate art is 1 in 1,994 English packs on TCGPlayer's Evolving Skies table. Six alternate-art VMAX in the set. Rayquaza VMAX alt is the same pull. People pay more for Umbreon. That is demand, not a rarer card.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`evolving-skies`,`umbreon`,`swsh`],cardId:`swsh7-215`},{id:`tcg-ray-same-slot`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Rayquaza VMAX alt is the same pull as Moonbreon`,stat:`1/1994`,sub:`Six alternate-art VMAX in Evolving Skies. Each one is about 1 in 1,994 packs.`,why:`TCGPlayer's table: a specific Evolving Skies alternate-art VMAX is 1 in 1,994 packs. Umbreon and Rayquaza are equal in that table. The hobby did not treat them as equal on the singles board. Print is not demand.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`evolving-skies`,`rayquaza`,`swsh`],cardId:`swsh7-218`},{id:`tcg-lost-origin`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Lost Origin brought the Lost Zone back as a main-set mechanic.`,stat:`LOR`,sub:`September 9, 2022. Giratina is the mascot.`,why:`Lost Origin is a Sword & Shield main set with a 36-pack box and a Trainer Gallery. Cards can put other cards into the Lost Zone, a pile that is not the discard. Giratina V alternate art is the famous chase. Lost Zone is not 'lost' as in missing from the pop report.`,image:`/games/lost-origin-giratina.jpg`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`lost-origin`,`giratina`,`swsh`,`swsh11`]},{id:`tcg-lost-zone`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The Lost Zone is a third pile, not the discard pile.`,stat:`LZ`,sub:`Cards there usually cannot come back.`,why:`When a Lost Origin card sends something to the Lost Zone, that card leaves play and discard. It is a rules pile with its own count, used by Giratina and Comfey lines in that format. Do not mix it with prizes, with exile from other games, or with a card you physically lost.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`lost-origin`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-giratina-alt`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Lost Origin Giratina V alternate art is a counted chase, not a Moonbreon.`,stat:`LOR`,sub:`Thicker than Moonbreon in TCGPlayer's samples.`,why:`TCGPlayer's Lost Origin article put Giratina V alt near one in 804 English packs, from an 8,000-pack sample. That is thinner than an early SV SIR and thicker than Evolving Skies alt VMAX. Same hobby. Different tables; do not borrow Moonbreon's 2,000.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`lost-origin`,`giratina`,`swsh11`],cardId:`swsh11-186`,image:`/games/lost-origin-giratina.jpg`},{id:`tcg-crown-zenith`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Crown Zenith closed Sword and Shield with a Galarian Gallery.`,stat:`CRZ`,sub:`January 20, 2023. Special set. No regular 36-pack box.`,why:`Crown Zenith is a special expansion wrapping Galar: ETBs, collections, and tins rather than a standard English booster display. The Galarian Gallery is its full-art subset, numbered GG. Gold VSTAR cards of the creation trio sit at the end. It is not Evolving Skies and not 151.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`crown-zenith`,`swsh`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-galarian-gallery`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Galarian Gallery cards use GG numbers, not TG numbers.`,stat:`GG`,sub:`Crown Zenith only. Trainer Gallery is a different subset.`,why:`Crown Zenith's Galarian Gallery is a full-art row numbered GG01 and up. Trainer Galleries from Brilliant Stars to Silver Tempest used TG numbers. Both are extra lists stapled to a parent set. A GG Pikachu is a Crown Zenith card; a TG Pikachu is not.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`crown-zenith`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-paldea-evolved`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Paldea Evolved is the second Scarlet and Violet main set.`,stat:`PAL`,sub:`June 9, 2023. Iono. A very large English list.`,why:`Paldea Evolved combined Japanese sets into a 279-card English expansion with a 36-pack box. Iono's special illustration rare is the trainer chase. TCGPlayer still had the SIR slot near one in 32 packs, with a long SIR list sharing it. Early SV math, big set.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`paldea-evolved`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-temporal-forces`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Temporal Forces split the set between Ancient and Future paradoxes.`,stat:`TEF`,sub:`March 22, 2024. ACE SPEC returns. Regulation H begins.`,why:`Temporal Forces is a main set with a 36-pack box. Walking Wake and Iron Leaves headline the two paradox teams. ACE SPEC cards return here. Play! Pokémon's H regulation mark starts with this wave, which is why people treat Temporal Forces as the first 'still in Standard' SV box after G rotated.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`temporal-forces`,`sv`,`rotation`,`ace-spec`]},{id:`tcg-shrouded-fable`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Shrouded Fable is a small special set around the Loyal Three.`,stat:`SFA`,sub:`August 2, 2024. Ninety-nine cards. No English booster box.`,why:`Shrouded Fable is a Scarlet & Violet special expansion: Pecharunt, Munkidori, Fezandipiti, Okidogi, and Kingambit. Special means ETBs and collections, not a 36-pack display. Mini-set is not the same as a main set with a short list. SKU still matters.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`shrouded-fable`,`sv`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-paradox-rift`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Paradox Rift is the paradox-Pokémon main set from November 2023.`,stat:`PAR`,sub:`Roaring Moon, Iron Valiant. Last big G-mark main set.`,why:`Paradox Rift is a 36-pack English main set packed with past and future paradox Pokémon. TCGPlayer later quoted its any-SIR slot near one in 47 packs, already thinner than the 1-in-32 early sets. G regulation cards from this set left in-person Standard on April 10, 2026.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`paradox-rift`,`sv`,`rotation`]},{id:`tcg-sv-base-2023`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Scarlet and Violet base set launched the era on March 31, 2023.`,stat:`SVI`,sub:`Koraidon, Miraidon, illustration rares, two reverse slots.`,why:`The first Scarlet & Violet expansion rebuilt pack anatomy and rarity. English booster boxes exist. Regulation G starts here. Koraidon ex and Miraidon ex are the box mascots; 151 and Prismatic are later specials in the same era, not reprints of this list.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-me-launch`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Mega Evolution opened a new TCG era on September 26, 2025.`,stat:`ME`,sub:`First English set of the block. One hundred eighty-eight cards.`,why:`Pokémon TCG: Mega Evolution is the first main expansion of the Mega Evolution Series, not a Scarlet & Violet special. English combined Japanese Mega Brave and Mega Symphonia. Ten Mega Evolution Pokémon ex headline it. The era is a block name, like XY or Sword & Shield.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`]},{id:`tcg-me-jp-source`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`English Mega Evolution is Mega Brave plus Mega Symphonia.`,stat:`JP+JP`,sub:`Japan released those two on August 1, 2025.`,why:`English sets often merge a pair of Japanese expansions. Mega Evolution is that merge: Mega Brave and Mega Symphonia, plus a handful of reprinted trainers with a new regulation mark. Japanese timing is weeks earlier. The English card count is the combined list, not one Japanese box.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`,`japanese`,`english`]},{id:`tcg-me-no-regular-ex`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The first Mega Evolution set prints no regular Pokémon ex.`,stat:`10 MEGA`,sub:`All ten ex in that set are Mega Evolutions.`,why:`Mega Evolution's English list uses Mega Evolution Pokémon ex instead of ordinary ex for its double-rare attackers, one per type in that first wave. Later Mega Evolution-era sets add regular ex again. If you are looking for a non-Mega ex in the first set, you are on the wrong checklist.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`,`ex`]},{id:`tcg-phantasmal-flames`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Phantasmal Flames is the second Mega Evolution main set.`,stat:`Nov 2025`,sub:`November 14, 2025. Mega Gengar sits in this wave.`,why:`Phantasmal Flames follows Mega Evolution in the new block. It is a main English expansion with a booster box, not a special Unova-style dual set. Mega Evolution-era sets keep SV pack anatomy. The block name on the wrapper is the era, not a mechanic you put in the deck.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`]},{id:`tcg-ascended-heroes`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Ascended Heroes is a large Mega Evolution special from January 2026.`,stat:`Jan 2026`,sub:`Special expansion. Not the first Mega Evolution main set.`,why:`Ascended Heroes released January 30, 2026 as a special in the Mega Evolution Series, with a much larger list than a typical main set. Special still means you check SKUs: ETBs, collections, whether a 36-pack box exists. Do not borrow 151's no-box rule without looking at this product line.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-perfect-order`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Perfect Order is a March 2026 Mega Evolution main set.`,stat:`Mar 2026`,sub:`March 27, 2026. Main expansion in the new block.`,why:`Perfect Order continues the Mega Evolution Series after Ascended Heroes. Main set means a standard English expansion cadence, usually with a 36-pack box. It is not 30th Celebration and not Black Bolt. Era first, product type second, card list third.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`]},{id:`tcg-chaos-rising`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Chaos Rising is a May 2026 Mega Evolution main set.`,stat:`May 2026`,sub:`May 22, 2026. Keep the era and the SKU separate.`,why:`Chaos Rising sits in the Mega Evolution block between Perfect Order and Pitch Black. New main sets keep adding letters and Mega ex. Rotation still keys off the regulation mark on the card, not off the words Chaos Rising on the booster wrap.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`,`rotation`]},{id:`tcg-pitch-black`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Pitch Black is a July 2026 Mega Evolution main set.`,stat:`Jul 2026`,sub:`July 17, 2026. English still follows a Japanese source set.`,why:`Pitch Black released in English on July 17, 2026, in the Mega Evolution Series. English still follows Japanese by weeks to months. Import timing is not a reprint calendar, and it is not a Standard rotation date.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`,`japanese`]},{id:`tcg-30th-celebration`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`30th Celebration is the 2026 anniversary set, not 2021 Celebrations.`,stat:`30`,sub:`September 16, 2026. Different set from Sword and Shield Celebrations.`,why:`Celebrations in 2021 marked 25 years inside Sword & Shield. 30th Celebration is a 2026 Mega Evolution-era anniversary product with its own list, including a run of Pikachu cards. Do not price or sleeve them as the same set. Twenty-five and thirty are different printings.`,image:`/games/30th-pikachu-01.jpg`,asOf:`2026-09-16`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`,`pikachu`,`30th-celebration`,`me55`]},{id:`tcg-celebrations-25`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Celebrations was the 25th anniversary mini-set in 2021.`,stat:`25`,sub:`Classic Collection reprints plus new gold cards.`,why:`Celebrations sat inside Sword & Shield as a small special expansion with a Classic Collection of famous reprints. It is not Evolving Skies, not 151, and not 30th Celebration. Anniversary sets are their own SKUs. The number in the name is the birthday, not a pull rate.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`,`celebrations`]},{id:`tcg-me-mirror-holo`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Mega Evolution brings back mirror holofoil from Black and White.`,stat:`MIRROR`,sub:`A foil treatment last used as a regular variant in BW.`,why:`The first Mega Evolution expansion reprints the mirror holofoil idea from the Black & White series as a variant finish. Finish is not rarity. A mirror holo of a main-set card is still that main-set card. Do not catalog it as a special illustration rare.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`,`bw`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-enhanced-box`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Some modern English sets ship an Enhanced booster box with a topper.`,stat:`TOPPER`,sub:`Journey Together and Mega Evolution used this SKU.`,why:`Alongside the regular 36-pack display, TPC has sold Enhanced booster boxes that include a stamped illustration-rare box topper. That is a different SKU from the plain box. The packs inside are still the set's packs. The topper is why the two boxes should not be averaged.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`journey-together`,`mega-evolution`]},{id:`tcg-emea-delay`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`English street dates can slip by region without changing the set.`,stat:`EMEA`,sub:`Mega Evolution's EMEA release moved after the US date.`,why:`Mega Evolution released September 26, 2025 in many regions, while Europe, the Middle East, and Africa waited until October 10 because of delivery problems. The card list did not change. A later street date is logistics. It is not a new print run name like Unlimited.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`mega-evolution`,`english`]},{id:`tcg-psa-10`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`PSA 10 means gem mint on that company's scale.`,stat:`10`,sub:`It is a grade of one card, not a print run.`,why:`Professional Sports Authenticator's 10 is their top number: gem mint centering, corners, edges, and surface. Other companies use other 10s. A PSA 10 is a slabbed opinion about one piece of cardboard. It does not change the set symbol or the pull rate.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`]},{id:`tcg-raw-means`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Raw means the card is not in a grading slab.`,stat:`RAW`,sub:`Raw can still be mint. It is not a PSA 1.`,why:`Raw is the hobby word for ungraded. A raw near-mint Charizard is a condition note, not a company score. Grading companies will still look at centering, corners, edges, and surface if you send it. Raw is not a rarity and not a print.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`]},{id:`tcg-pop-is-census`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A pop report counts graded copies, not cards printed.`,stat:`POP`,sub:`It grows when more copies are submitted.`,why:`PSA's population report is a census of cards that company has graded, broken down by grade. It is not The Pokémon Company's print quantity. If a thousand more 151 Charizards get sent in next month, the pop rises. The print run did not magically grow by a thousand.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`]},{id:`tcg-pop-grows`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Population numbers only go up as people keep submitting.`,stat:`POP`,sub:`A low pop today can be a higher pop next year.`,why:`Every new slab adds to the census. Modern print quality also 10s more often than 1999 Wizards stock, so modern pops can look huge without meaning TPC printed more of that card than Base Set. Read pop as 'how many have been graded,' then stop.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`,`vintage`]},{id:`tcg-pop-not-scarcity`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A high pop 10 can still be a scarce pack pull.`,stat:`POP`,sub:`People grade chases. They do not grade every common.`,why:`Moonbreon and 151 Charizard have large PSA populations because owners send expensive cards. Bulk commons from the same packs barely appear. Pop is a map of what people slab, not a map of what left the factory. Use pull tables for packs; use pop for slabs.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`,`sir`]},{id:`tcg-centering-corners`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Grades look at centering, corners, edges, and surface.`,stat:`C/C/E/S`,sub:`Whitening on a back edge is enough to miss a 10.`,why:`The four sides of a grade are physical. A perfectly rare special illustration rare with a dinged corner is not a 10. Vintage Wizards stock also has print-quality issues that modern cards do not. Rarity does not protect a corner.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`]},{id:`tcg-qualifier`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`PSA qualifiers like OC describe a defect the number did not swallow.`,stat:`OC`,sub:`Off-center, marks, stain. The number still sits on the label.`,why:`A qualifier is a tag such as OC for off-center or MK for marks. It tells you why the card did not get a clean number, or that a problem exists beside the number. Qualifiers are not set symbols. They are grading language about that copy.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`]},{id:`tcg-other-graders`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`BGS and CGC are other graders, not worse PSA.`,stat:`BGS`,sub:`A BGS 10 is not a PSA 10 with a different logo.`,why:`Beckett and Certified Guaranty Company each have their own 10, their own labels, and their own subgrades. Beckett's black label is a perfect 10 on every subgrade. Cross-company apples-to-apples is a hobby argument. Catch'em treats them as different books.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`]},{id:`tcg-someone-elses-10`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A 10 on someone else's copy is not a 10 on yours.`,stat:`SLAB`,sub:`Pulse never grades the card in your binder.`,why:`Sold slabs and pop reports describe cards that already went through a company. Your raw copy has not. Centering can differ from pack to pack on the same print plate. Look at the corners in front of you; do not borrow a stranger's 10.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`]},{id:`tcg-1st-ed-not-grade`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`First Edition and gem mint are two different axes.`,stat:`1ST≠10`,sub:`You can have a damaged First Edition. You can have a 10 Unlimited.`,why:`Print run is what left the factory: stamp or no stamp, shadow or no shadow. Grade is the condition of this piece after 25 years in a binder. A First Edition 5 is still First Edition. An Unlimited 10 is still Unlimited; do not let the slab title collapse those words.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`,`vintage`,`base-set`]},{id:`tcg-auth-not-grade`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Some slabs only authenticate. They do not score 1 through 10.`,stat:`AUTH`,sub:`Error cards often travel this way.`,why:`Companies can encapsulate a card as authentic without putting a numeric grade on it, especially for obvious errors or altered cards. Authentic means 'this is a real card' in that company's opinion. It is not gem mint, and it is not a secret rare rank.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`,`error`]},{id:`tcg-resubmit`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A pop report can include cards that were cracked and sent back.`,stat:`RESUB`,sub:`The census is submissions, not unique copies of Earth.`,why:`Collectors crack slabs and resubmit, hoping for a higher number. Population reports are not a perfect unique-card census. They are still the best public count of graded copies, which is why they get quoted. They remain a grade census, not a print run.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`]},{id:`tcg-standard-letter`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Standard rotation keys off the letter on the card.`,stat:`H I J`,sub:`Not the expansion name. Not a print-stop.`,why:`Play! Pokémon Standard uses regulation marks. After April 10, 2026, in-person Standard is H, I, J, and later letters. A card from a new set can still print an old letter and be illegal. Read the mark by the artist credit; the booster wrap is not the rulebook.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`rotation`]},{id:`tcg-g-left`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`G-mark cards left in-person Standard on April 10, 2026.`,stat:`G`,sub:`TCG Live flipped two weeks earlier, on March 26.`,why:`Play! Pokémon announced the 2026 rotation on January 9, 2026: G drops out and H, I, and J stay. Digital Standard on TCG Live rotated March 26 so players could test, then paper events moved April 10. Two calendars, one letter.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`rotation`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-rotation-not-print`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Rotation is play legality. It is not TPC ending a print run.`,stat:`PLAY`,sub:`A rotated set can still sit on a warehouse pallet.`,why:`When G left Standard, 151 and Obsidian Flames did not become officially out of print. The Pokémon Company does not publish a reprint calendar on rotation day. Tournament legality and factory status are different offices. Do not treat April 10 as a print-stop stamp.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`rotation`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-reprint-new-letter`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A reprint can pick up a new regulation mark and stay legal.`,stat:`I`,sub:`Rare Candy from Sun and Moon still plays via a Mega Evolution reprint.`,why:`Play! Pokémon's own 2026 example: Sun & Moon Rare Candy is usable because Mega Evolution printed Rare Candy with an I mark. Older copies of a legal card name can still be played. The letter on a current printing is the permission slip, not the copyright date.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`rotation`,`mega-evolution`]},{id:`tcg-g-year-sets`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The G year is Scarlet and Violet through Paradox Rift, plus 151.`,stat:`G`,sub:`Useful shorthand. Mixed-mark specials still need a look.`,why:`Scarlet & Violet base, Paldea Evolved, Obsidian Flames, 151, and Paradox Rift are the G-mark mains and the 151 special. Paldean Fates sits in that year too. Later reprints can mix marks in one product. Shorthand helps sealed talk; the letter still wins at a tournament.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`rotation`,`sv`,`151`]},{id:`tcg-h-shorthand`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`People treat Temporal Forces through Prismatic as the H year.`,stat:`H`,sub:`H is in Standard. G is not. Check mixed products anyway.`,why:`Temporal Forces, Twilight Masquerade, Shrouded Fable, Stellar Crown, Surging Sparks, and Prismatic Evolutions are the boxes players shorthand as H. Journey Together and later pick up I. Useful for a binder. Illegal if you skip the letter on a reprint.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`rotation`,`temporal-forces`,`prismatic`]},{id:`tcg-two-week-wait`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`New cards wait about two weeks before they are tournament legal.`,stat:`14 D`,sub:`Street date is not event date.`,why:`Play! Pokémon typically adds a new expansion to Standard two weeks after release. Prerelease promos have their own rules. A card you opened Friday night is not automatically legal at Saturday's Cup. Check the current Play! Pokémon announcement, not the shrink wrap.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-deck-60`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A constructed deck is sixty cards.`,stat:`60`,sub:`No more than four copies of a card, except Basic Energy.`,why:`Standard, Expanded, and most constructed formats build 60. You may play four copies of a card name, with Basic Energy unlimited and ACE SPEC limited to one. Prize cards come out of that 60. A 40-card Limited deck from a prerelease is a different format.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-six-prizes`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Each player sets six prize cards at the start of a game.`,stat:`6`,sub:`Take prizes when you knock out the other Pokémon.`,why:`After the opening setup, six cards from your deck become prizes. Knock out a Pokémon, take one. Knock out an ex, take two. Knock out a Mega Evolution ex, take three; First to take all remaining prizes, or to empty the other deck, wins under the usual rules.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-supporter-once`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`You may play one Supporter card per turn.`,stat:`1`,sub:`Items do not have that cap. ACE SPEC still has its own cap.`,why:`Supporters are the once-per-turn trainers: Iono, Professor's Research, Boss's Orders. Items can be played in multiples, subject to their text. Stadiums replace each other. ACE SPEC is a separate one-per-deck limit; three different valves on the same trainer stack.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-expanded`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Expanded is a wider Play! Pokémon format than Standard.`,stat:`EXP`,sub:`Older letters stay. Ban lists still exist.`,why:`Expanded keeps a much longer card pool so Black & White-onward cards can show up with later ones. It is not Unlimited vintage Wizards. It is not Standard. Rotation news about G leaving Standard does not by itself kick G out of Expanded.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-limited`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Prerelease Build and Battle is Limited, not Standard.`,stat:`40`,sub:`You build from the box in front of you.`,why:`Build & Battle boxes at prereleases include a 40-card ready deck plus packs to add. The deck size and the legal pool are the product in your hands. A prerelease stamp promo is often not Standard-legal that weekend. Do not sleeve it into a Cup deck without checking.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-jp-first-timing`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Japanese sets almost always release before English.`,stat:`JP→EN`,sub:`Weeks to a few months. The English list is often a merge.`,why:`The Pokémon Company reveals Japanese expansions first. English follows, often combining two Japanese sets into one larger English list. 151's gap was about three months. Mega Evolution's gap was about eight weeks; English street date is not the Japanese print date.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`japanese`,`english`]},{id:`tcg-en-merges`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`One English main set is often two Japanese sets stapled together.`,stat:`2→1`,sub:`Temporal Forces is Wild Force plus Cyber Judge.`,why:`English Paldea Evolved, Temporal Forces, Twilight Masquerade, and Mega Evolution each combined a Japanese pair. That is why English lists look longer than a single Japanese box. Cards cut from English can return as Elite Trainer Box promos. The merge is a localization choice.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`english`,`japanese`,`temporal-forces`]},{id:`tcg-jp-high-class`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Japanese high-class packs are a different product from English specials.`,stat:`HIGH`,sub:`Ten Japanese cards, ball reverses, different guarantees.`,why:`Japanese 151, Shiny Treasure ex, and VSTAR Universe are high-class packs: more cards per pack than a Japanese main, Poké Ball and Master Ball reverses, denser hits. English 151 and Paldean Fates are the localizations, not photocopies of those wrappers. Odds tables do not copy across the ocean.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`japanese`,`151`,`paldean-fates`]},{id:`tcg-jp-larger-list`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The Japanese list can be larger than the English special.`,stat:`360>245`,sub:`Shiny Treasure ex outran English Paldean Fates.`,why:`Japanese Shiny Treasure ex numbered hundreds more cards than English Paldean Fates, which dropped some shinies and folded in other leftovers. Master-set goals do not match 1:1. If a Japanese chase has no English number, it is a Japanese card, not a missing English slot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`japanese`,`english`,`paldean-fates`]},{id:`tcg-cut-cards`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`English can cut a Japanese illustration rare and print it as a promo.`,stat:`CUT`,sub:`Mega Evolution turned two Japanese IRs into ETB promos.`,why:`Localization is not a full reprint of the Japanese checklist. Mega Evolution's English set cut the Riolu and Alakazam illustration rares from the Japanese pair and used them as Elite Trainer Box promos. 'Not in the English set' can still mean 'in the English product line.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`english`,`japanese`,`mega-evolution`]},{id:`tcg-language-legal`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Foreign-language cards can be legal if you can reference the English text.`,stat:`LANG`,sub:`Play! Pokémon allows it with an official English proxy of the wording.`,why:`Organized play lets you use Japanese, French, or other printings of a legal card as long as the card itself is a real Pokémon TCG card and you can show the current English text. The judge is not grading your language. The letter and the name still have to match a legal card.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`japanese`,`english`]},{id:`tcg-text-errata`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Card text can receive errata after print.`,stat:`ERRATA`,sub:`Play the current Play! Pokémon wording, not the first ink.`,why:`Attacks and abilities sometimes change in the official database after a set ships. Tournament play uses the current text. Collectors still catalog the ink on the paper. Those two hobbies share a card and do not share a rulebook; check the Compendium when it matters.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-set-code-live`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Three-letter set codes come from TCG Live, not from the rarity icon.`,stat:`OBF`,sub:`MEW is 151. PAL is Paldea Evolved. EVS is Evolving Skies.`,why:`The code printed for digital redemption — SVI, PAL, OBF, MEW, PAF, TEF, TWM, SSP, PRE, JTG — is how players shorthand a printing. It is not a regulation mark. MEW for 151 is a code, not a statement that every card is Mew.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`live`,`151`,`obsidian-flames`]},{id:`tcg-upc-not-etb`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`An Ultra Premium Collection is not a large Elite Trainer Box.`,stat:`UPC`,sub:`More packs, metal or exclusive promos, display pieces.`,why:`UPCs are premium SKUs with a higher pack count and extra display items. The 151 UPC is sixteen packs. An ETB is nine in Scarlet & Violet. Promos inside a UPC can be exclusive; do not use UPC pack math on an ETB, and do not call a UPC a booster box.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`upc`,`151`]},{id:`tcg-special-means-sku`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Special expansion means the pack SKUs changed, not that the cards are fake.`,stat:`SPCL`,sub:`No 36-pack box is the usual tell in English.`,why:`151, Paldean Fates, Shrouded Fable, Prismatic Evolutions, and Black Bolt / White Flare are special English expansions. The cards are real Standard-era cards. The missing product is the 36-pack display. Hunt and sealed talk has to start with which wrapper TPC actually sold.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`151`,`prismatic`,`paldean-fates`]},{id:`tcg-reprint-unknown`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`TPC does not publish a reprint calendar.`,stat:`NONE`,sub:`Until they post a print run, do not invent one.`,why:`The Pokémon Company does not announce when a set's last sheet leaves the plant. Rotation is not that announcement. Sold-out at retail is not that announcement. Catch'em will not invent a print-stop date for 151, Evolving Skies, or anything else.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`reprint`]},{id:`tcg-bb-not-etb`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A booster box and an Elite Trainer Box are different products.`,stat:`BB≠ETB`,sub:`Thirty-six packs versus eight or nine, plus accessories in the ETB.`,why:`The display box is packs only, usually 36 in English mains. The Elite Trainer Box is a hobby kit: packs, sleeves, dice, energy, a cardboard box, a promo. Per-pack cost, promo value, and hunt math all change. Calling both 'a box' is how people lose count.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`etb`]},{id:`tcg-sleeved-vs-unsleeved`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Sleeved boosters and retail unsleeved packs are the same set table.`,stat:`SLEEVE`,sub:`The extra plastic is packaging, not a better slot.`,why:`English sleeved boosters add a clear wrapper for hanging on a peg. The cards inside follow the same slot table as unsleeved packs of that set. Art on the sleeve is still not a promised Pokémon. Do not pay a rarity premium for the plastic.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`pack`]},{id:`tcg-slot-vs-specific`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Any-SIR rate is not the rate of the Charizard you wanted.`,stat:`SLOT`,sub:`Divide by how many SIRs share the slot.`,why:`If any special illustration rare is one in 32 packs and six SIRs share that slot, a specific SIR lands near one in 192. Obsidian Flames is that homework. People quote the slot when they want the set to feel kind, and the specific rate when they want the chase to feel cruel. Use both words.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sir`,`obsidian-flames`]},{id:`tcg-rate-is-pack`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Published hunt rates are per pack, not per Elite Trainer Box.`,stat:`PACK`,sub:`Nine packs is nine rolls, not one.`,why:`TCGPlayer Authentication Center rates are pack rates. An SV ETB is nine independent packs, and a 36-pack box is 36 rolls, not one promised SIR. Expected-value talk starts with packs; it does not end as a promise.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`etb`,`sir`]},{id:`tcg-auth-center-sample`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`TCGPlayer Authentication Center rates are samples, not TPC sheets.`,stat:`SAMPLE`,sub:`Official Pokémon does not publish English 1-in-N tables.`,why:`The 1-in-32 and 1-in-87 SIR slots, Moonbreon at 1 in 2,000, and 151 Charizard at 1 in 225 come from counted packs, not from a factory manifesto. Confidence intervals on a specific rare can be wide. They are the honest public table. They are still a sample.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sir`,`151`,`evolving-skies`]},{id:`tcg-do-not-invent-rate`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`If a set has no counted table, Catch'em will not invent a 1-in-N.`,stat:`NO 1/N`,sub:`Mega Evolution and Destined Rivals do not get a fake Moonbreon number.`,why:`Moonbreon, 151 Charizard SIR, early SV SIR slots, later SV SIR slots, and Obsidian's specific SIR are sourced. Newer sets without an Authentication Center article do not inherit those numbers. 'It felt like 1 in 200' is a story. It is not a rate.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sir`,`mega-evolution`,`destined-rivals`]},{id:`tcg-demand-not-print`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Hobby price follows demand. The slot does not move because people like Umbreon.`,stat:`DEMAND`,sub:`Moonbreon and Rayquaza VMAX alt are the same class.`,why:`Six Evolving Skies alt VMAX share one table. Umbreon took the nickname and the singles board, but Rayquaza is the same pull. Learn the slot before you learn the hype: Paldean Fates shiny volume is thick, and a specific later SIR can be thinner.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`evolving-skies`,`umbreon`,`rayquaza`],cardId:`swsh7-215`},{id:`tcg-weakness-x2`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Modern weakness is usually double damage, not plus 30.`,stat:`×2`,sub:`Base Set used plus 30. The math changed.`,why:`Wizards Base Set printed weakness as +30. Later TCG printings moved to ×2 for most Pokémon. A 100-damage attack becomes 200 against weakness in modern play. Vintage and modern cards in the same binder do not share a weakness rule if you actually play them.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`vintage`,`base-set`]},{id:`tcg-resistance`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Resistance is usually 30 less damage.`,stat:`-30`,sub:`It sits under weakness on the card.`,why:`If a Pokémon resists the attacker's type, subtract 30 after other modifiers in the usual modern order. Not every Pokémon has resistance. Colorless attackers often see resistance on the defending card. Resistance is a combat stat, not a rarity.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-retreat`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Retreat cost is how much energy you pay to bench the Pokémon.`,stat:`RETREAT`,sub:`Some cards retreat for free. Some never want to.`,why:`The retreat number in the corner is colorless energy you must discard to move that Pokémon to the bench, unless an effect says otherwise. High-HP ex often have heavy retreat. That is a play tax, not a collector rank. A 0-retreat attacker is a deckbuilding fact.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-colorless-energy`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A colorless energy cost can be paid with any energy.`,stat:`C`,sub:`Typed costs need that type. Colorless is the wild slot.`,why:`Attacks show typed energy and colorless energy. Colorless does not mean Colorless-type Pokémon; it means any energy pays that part of the cost. Basic Darkness cannot pay a Fire cost, but it can pay a colorless cost, which is how beginners brick an attack.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`energy`]},{id:`tcg-special-energy`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Special Energy is not Basic Energy and is limited to four copies.`,stat:`SPL`,sub:`ACE SPEC Energy is stricter: one in the whole deck.`,why:`Basic Energy is unlimited. Double Colorless, Reversal, Luminous, and other Special Energy cards follow the four-copy rule unless they are also ACE SPEC. Pack energy at the back of a booster is Basic. A Special Energy you pulled from a reverse slot is a different card.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`energy`,`ace-spec`]},{id:`tcg-basic-stage`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Basic, Stage 1, and Stage 2 are TCG evolution stages, not National Dex lines only.`,stat:`STAGE`,sub:`Some Baby Pokémon and Restored Pokémon add extra lanes.`,why:`A Stage 2 in the TCG is a card that evolves from a Stage 1. That usually matches the games, but Restored Pokémon from fossils and some Baby Pokémon in older sets extra-step the line. You cannot play a Stage 2 as a Basic unless an effect says so. The stage is printed at the top.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-ability-vs-attack`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Abilities are not attacks. You do not pay energy to use most of them.`,stat:`ABL`,sub:`Once-per-turn limits live in the text.`,why:`Attacks have an energy cost and happen in the attack step. Abilities, and older Poké-Powers and Poké-Bodies, follow their own sentences. Iono is a Supporter, not an Ability. If you skip that vocabulary, you will misplay rotation, you will misplay prizes, and you will mis-sort a binder.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-tool`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Pokémon Tool is a trainer you attach, one per Pokémon by default.`,stat:`TOOL`,sub:`ACE SPEC Tools still also obey the one-ACE-SPEC deck rule.`,why:`Tools sit on a Pokémon like a held item. The default rule is one Tool per Pokémon. Some cards raise that. A Tool is not an Energy and not a Stadium; prime Catcher and other ACE SPEC Tools still count as your one ACE SPEC for the deck.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`ace-spec`]},{id:`tcg-stadium`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Only one Stadium is in play. The new one discards the old one.`,stat:`STAD`,sub:`Playing a Stadium on top of yours can be a waste.`,why:`Stadiums are trainers that sit in the shared middle. A new Stadium of a different name discards the current one. ACE SPEC Stadiums exist in Scarlet & Violet. Art on a Stadium illustration rare is still a Stadium when you play it; Binder rank and play type are different labels.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-four-copy`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Four copies of a card name is the constructed limit.`,stat:`4`,sub:`Basic Energy ignores it. ACE SPEC is one.`,why:`Card name, not art, is what the four-copy rule sees. Different printings of Iono still count as Iono. Illustration rare and regular rare of the same Magikarp are the same name if the name line matches. People building master sets ignore this; people building decks cannot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-condition-words`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Near mint, lightly played, and damaged are seller condition, not PSA numbers.`,stat:`NM`,sub:`A seller's NM is not a PSA 10.`,why:`Marketplaces use NM, LP, MP, HP, and damaged as a raw-condition scale. Those letters are house style. They are not a 1–10 slab. A near-mint raw card can still fail a 10 for centering; do not translate NM into PSA 9 in your head.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`]},{id:`tcg-counterfeit`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A counterfeit is not an error card.`,stat:`FAKE`,sub:`Errors come from the plant. Fakes come from somewhere else.`,why:`Miscuts and ink skips happen on real sheets. Counterfeits copy a chase to fool a buyer. Real errors still have real backs, real stock, and usually a plausible cut from a sheet. If the back color, the font, or the light test is wrong, you are not holding a factory misprint.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`error`]},{id:`tcg-holo-layer`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Holofoil is a layer. Texture is another layer.`,stat:`FOIL`,sub:`Ultra rares add texture. Reverse holos move the foil to the border.`,why:`A vintage Wizards holo puts foil under the art window. A reverse holo puts foil on the body and leaves the art quieter. Scarlet & Violet ultra rares add a tactile texture. Those are manufacturing choices; none of them is a PSA grade, and none of them is a regulation mark.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`vintage`]},{id:`tcg-silver-border`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Sword and Shield and Scarlet and Violet use a silver face border.`,stat:`SILVER`,sub:`Wizards English used yellow. The back stayed Pokémon.`,why:`Modern English cards dropped the vintage yellow ring for a silver-grey frame. Japanese cards never used that English yellow face. When a listing says 'yellow border Charizard,' it is pointing at Wizards-era English, not at a 151 special illustration rare.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`swsh`,`sv`,`english`]},{id:`tcg-set-symbol`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The set symbol in the corner names the expansion, not the era.`,stat:`SYMBOL`,sub:`Base Set has no symbol. Jungle's flower is the classic tell.`,why:`Base Set English cards have no expansion symbol. Jungle adds a flower, Fossil a fossil, Team Rocket an R. Modern cards always show a symbol plus a number. The symbol is how you separate XY Evolutions Charizard from Base Set Charizard without reading the copyright.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`base-set`,`jungle`]},{id:`tcg-artist-credit`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The illustrator name is printed on every modern card.`,stat:`ARTIST`,sub:`Arita, Sugimori, and hundreds of later names.`,why:`Ken Sugimori defined the game look. Mitsuhiro Arita defined early TCG holos. Illustration rares exist because TPC hired painters for full-card scenes. Two cards of the same Pokémon with different artists are different collectibles even when the attacks match.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-mcdonalds`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`McDonald's promos are real cards from a Happy Meal, not booster hits.`,stat:`MCD`,sub:`Different stamp, different product, often cosmos holo.`,why:`Annual McDonald's collections print stamped or cosmos-holo Pokémon in kids' meals. They are official TPC cards. They are not secret rares from a 36-pack box. File them as promos; a McDonald's Pikachu is not Base Set 58/102 and not a Surging Sparks SIR.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`promo`,`pikachu`]},{id:`tcg-black-star-promo`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Black-star promos use a star in place of a set number.`,stat:`SWSH001`,sub:`Stamped 'Promo.' Not a secret rare from a pack slot.`,why:`Promos numbered SWSH001, SVP, or older black-star numbers come from tins, boxes, League, and magazines. The black star is the promo mark. A stamped Elite Trainer Box promo is this family. It can be chase cardboard; it is not an illustration-rare pack slot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`promo`]},{id:`tcg-first-movie-promos`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`US theaters handed out four Wizards promos with the first movie.`,stat:`1999`,sub:`Pikachu, Mewtwo, Electabuzz, Dragonite. Not Ancient Mew.`,why:`The 1999 North American theatrical run of the first Pokémon movie gave a random Wizards promo from a four-card group. Ancient Mew belongs to the later Power of One movie. Movie cardboard is a promo. It is not a Base Set pack hit and not a 151 SIR.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`promo`,`vintage`,`mewtwo`]},{id:`tcg-ancient-mew-promo`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Ancient Mew is a movie promo with unique script, not a Base Set card.`,stat:`2000`,sub:`First week of The Power of One in US theaters.`,why:`US theaters gave Ancient Mew to first-week viewers of The Power of One in July 2000, then switched to legendary birds. Japan had placed Ancient Mew in pamphlets the year before. The hieroglyphic attacks are flavor. It is not Mew ex from 151.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`promo`,`vintage`,`mew`]},{id:`tcg-league-promo`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`League stamps mark participation cards, not pack rarity.`,stat:`LEAGUE`,sub:`A stamped Pikachu is still that Pikachu, plus a stamp.`,why:`Play! Pokémon League and older Wizards League issued stamped cards. The stamp is an event mark. It is not First Edition and not a special illustration rare. Condition still matters; a heavy-played League stamp is a played card with extra ink.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`promo`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-jumbo`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Jumbo cards are oversized promos. They are not tournament legal.`,stat:`JUMBO`,sub:`Collection boxes toss them in as display cardboard.`,why:`Oversize cards from Ultra Premium Collections and figure collections are souvenirs. You cannot put a jumbo in a 60-card deck. A jumbo Charizard is not the playable Charizard ex with the same art shrunk down, even when the illustration matches.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`promo`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-metal-card`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Metal cards in Ultra Premium Collections are souvenirs, not Standard cards.`,stat:`METAL`,sub:`The 151 UPC Mew is the famous example.`,why:`Some UPCs include a metal replica. It is not a legal constructed card and not a special illustration rare, even if it copies the art. The playable version, if there is one, is the paper card in the same product or in the set. Do not sleeve metal into a Cup deck.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`promo`,`151`,`upc`]},{id:`tcg-brilliant-stars`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Brilliant Stars introduced VSTAR and the Trainer Gallery.`,stat:`BRS`,sub:`February 25, 2022. Arceus VSTAR is the mascot.`,why:`Brilliant Stars is a Sword & Shield main set with a 36-pack box. VSTAR as a mechanic and TG-numbered Trainer Gallery cards start here. It sits between Fusion Strike and Astral Radiance. It is not Crown Zenith, which later gold-stamped the creation trio in a special set.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-fusion-strike`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Fusion Strike is a three-type fusion set from November 2021.`,stat:`FST`,sub:`Mew VMAX is the famous chase. Huge English list.`,why:`Fusion Strike printed Fusion Strike-style cards that wanted other Fusion Strike cards, in a very large Sword & Shield main set. Mew VMAX alternate art is the poster chase. Large lists thin specific secrets. Do not borrow Evolving Skies' 1-in-2,000 for this set without a table.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`,`mew`]},{id:`tcg-chilling-reign`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Chilling Reign split Galarian legendary birds across the set.`,stat:`CRE`,sub:`June 18, 2021. Shadow Rider and Ice Rider Calyrex.`,why:`Chilling Reign is a Sword & Shield main set with Galarian Articuno, Zapdos, and Moltres plus the two Calyrex riders. Alternate arts exist here too. It is not Evolving Skies. Bird legendary does not mean Fossil set, and it does not mean 151 Zapdos SIR.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-battle-styles`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Battle Styles opened 2021 with Single Strike and Rapid Strike.`,stat:`BST`,sub:`March 19, 2021. Urshifu is the mascot pair.`,why:`Battle Styles is a Sword & Shield main set that split decks into Single Strike and Rapid Strike tags, the way later SV would tag Ancient and Future. Tags are play lines. They are not rarity. An Elite Trainer Box from this era still holds eight packs.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-shining-fates`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Shining Fates is the Sword and Shield shiny special set.`,stat:`SHF`,sub:`February 19, 2021. Shiny Vault. No regular 36-pack box.`,why:`Shining Fates follows Hidden Fates' homework: a special English SKU plan, a Shiny Vault subset, Charizard as the poster shiny. It is not Paldean Fates, even though both words rhyme and both mean shiny. Paldean Fates is Scarlet & Violet. Shining Fates is Sword & Shield.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`,`sealed`,`charizard`]},{id:`tcg-champions-path`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Champion's Path is a small Sword and Shield special around Charizard.`,stat:`CPA`,sub:`September 2020. Special SKUs, not a main box.`,why:`Champion's Path is an early Sword & Shield special expansion with a famous Charizard V and VMAX. Special English products, not a 36-pack main display. It is not Hidden Fates, not Shining Fates, and not 151. Four different Charizard products, four different lists.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`,`charizard`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-pokemon-go-set`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The Pokémon GO TCG set is a 2022 special, not the phone game.`,stat:`PGO`,sub:`July 1, 2022. Eighty-eight cards plus Radiant pieces.`,why:`Pokémon TCG: Pokémon GO is a Sword & Shield special expansion themed on the mobile game. Packs of this set are paper. They do not unlock a raid in the app. Pocket, GO, and paper TCG are three products; pull tables do not copy across them.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`,`go`]},{id:`tcg-silver-tempest`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Silver Tempest closed the Sword and Shield main-set run.`,stat:`SIT`,sub:`November 11, 2022. Lugia V alt is the chase.`,why:`Silver Tempest is the last numbered Sword & Shield main expansion before Crown Zenith's special sendoff. Lugia V alternate art is the headline secret. TCGPlayer counted that specific alt near one in 674 packs, thicker than Moonbreon, still a thin secret.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`],cardId:`swsh12-186`},{id:`tcg-astral-radiance`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Astral Radiance brought Hisui and the first Radiant Pokémon.`,stat:`ASR`,sub:`May 27, 2022. Trainer Gallery continues.`,why:`Astral Radiance is a Sword & Shield main set themed on Legends: Arceus / Hisui. Radiant Pokémon as a one-per-deck frame start here. It is not Lost Origin, even though both sit in late SWSH. Hisui forms on paper are still SWSH cards, not Scarlet & Violet pack odds.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-vivid-voltage`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Vivid Voltage printed Amazing Rares with a cosmos-style frame.`,stat:`VIV`,sub:`November 13, 2020. Pikachu Amazing Rare is the poster.`,why:`Amazing Rare is a Vivid Voltage-era secret with a unique foil window. It is not an illustration rare, not a rainbow, and not a Gold Star. The mechanic did not continue as a regular SV rank. When someone says Amazing Pikachu, they mean this set, not Surging Sparks.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`,`pikachu`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-darkness-ablaze`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Darkness Ablaze is an early Sword and Shield Charizard VMAX set.`,stat:`DAA`,sub:`August 14, 2020. Main set, 36-pack box.`,why:`Darkness Ablaze printed Charizard VMAX in the main numbered set, plus a secret rainbow. That VMAX is a 2020 Sword & Shield card. It is not Base Set, not Champion's Path, not 151, and not Obsidian Flames. Ask which Charizard before you talk slots.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`,`charizard`]},{id:`tcg-cosmic-eclipse`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Cosmic Eclipse closed Sun and Moon with a giant Tag Team list.`,stat:`CEC`,sub:`November 1, 2019. Last SM main set.`,why:`Cosmic Eclipse is a 271-card Sun & Moon main expansion packed with Tag Team GX. It has a 36-pack English box. Hidden Fates had already arrived as a special a few months earlier. Main versus special is the SKU lesson inside a single era.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sm`]},{id:`tcg-team-up`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Team Up introduced Tag Team GX in February 2019.`,stat:`TEU`,sub:`Two Pokémon, one card, usually three prizes.`,why:`Team Up is a Sun & Moon main set. Latias & Latios GX and Pikachu & Zekrom GX are the faces of the Tag Team idea. Three-prize Tag Teams are a play tax you will see again on Mega Evolution ex. The frame and the letter on the card are what changed.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sm`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-burning-shadows`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Burning Shadows is an early Sun and Moon Charizard GX set.`,stat:`BUS`,sub:`August 4, 2017. Main set.`,why:`Burning Shadows printed Charizard GX, including a full-art and a rainbow secret. GX is the Sun & Moon attacker. This Charizard is not a VMAX and not an ex. Era frames exist so you can sort a stack of Charizard without a market app.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sm`,`charizard`]},{id:`tcg-generations`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`XY Generations is a 2016 special with a Radiant Collection.`,stat:`GEN`,sub:`Twentieth anniversary. Not XY Evolutions, not Celebrations.`,why:`Generations released as a special XY-era expansion with a Radiant Collection RC subset. Evolutions, later that year, is the Base Set tribute main set. Two 2016 products, two jobs. Radiant Collection is not Sword & Shield Radiant Pokémon.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`xy`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-phantom-forces`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Phantom Forces printed the first English Night March pieces.`,stat:`PHF`,sub:`November 5, 2014. Hex Maniac. Main XY set.`,why:`Phantom Forces is an XY main set remembered for playable trainers and for small Pokémon that powered Night March. Play history and collector history can ignore each other. A cheap play uncommon from 2014 is still a Phantom Forces card when you catalog it.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`xy`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-plasma-storm`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Team Plasma's TCG story sits in Plasma Storm, Freeze, and Blast.`,stat:`PLS`,sub:`2013. Black and White block, not Team Rocket 2000.`,why:`Plasma Storm, Plasma Freeze, and Plasma Blast are Black & White main sets with Team Plasma Pokémon and ACE SPEC in the later ones. Team Plasma is Unova. Team Rocket is Kanto. Destined Rivals is a 2025 Rocket set; three villains, three eras, three frames.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`bw`,`destined-rivals`]},{id:`tcg-boundaries-crossed`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Boundaries Crossed is the 2012 set that invented ACE SPEC.`,stat:`BCR`,sub:`Black and White block. Item ACE SPECs, one per deck.`,why:`Boundaries Crossed printed the first ACE SPEC cards and a large Black & White list. Temporal Forces in 2024 is a revival, not a reprint of those thirteen Items. If a deck list says ACE SPEC, check the era. The one-per-deck law is the part that survived.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`bw`,`ace-spec`]},{id:`tcg-call-of-legends`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Call of Legends closed HeartGold SoulSilver in 2011.`,stat:`CL`,sub:`Shiny legendary reprints. Last pre-Black-and-White English set.`,why:`Call of Legends mixed HGSS mechanics with a shiny legendary subset. Black & White base set arrived two months later and restarted the look. CL is an era hinge. It is not Crown Zenith and not Lost Origin, even though legendary is in the name.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`hgss`]},{id:`tcg-ex-power-keepers`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Power Keepers ended the EX era in 2007.`,stat:`PK`,sub:`Last English EX set before Diamond and Pearl.`,why:`Power Keepers closed the EX block, including leftover Gold Star energy. Diamond & Pearl base set followed with a new look, LV.X, and ten-card packs. Era hinges matter because frames, pack counts, and First Edition rules all change at the hinge.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`ex-era`,`dp`]},{id:`tcg-ruby-sapphire`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`EX Ruby and Sapphire is the first Pokémon Company English set.`,stat:`RS`,sub:`2003. No Wizards stamp. Pokémon-EX begins this era.`,why:`Ruby & Sapphire opened Hoenn on paper and opened TPC's English printing. The e-Card stripe is gone. First Edition is gone. Pokémon-EX is the new two-prize body; when someone says 'WOTC versus modern,' this set is the door.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`ex-era`]},{id:`tcg-skyridge`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Skyridge is the last Wizards English expansion.`,stat:`SKR`,sub:`2003. Crystal types. e-Reader stripe. Thin print reputation.`,why:`Skyridge closed the Wizards license with Crystal Pokémon and an e-Reader strip. Collectors treat it as a scarce late WOTC set. That reputation is about print and survival, not about a TCGPlayer Authentication Center pack table. Vintage still has no sourced 1-in-N in Catch'em.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`ecard`,`wizards`]},{id:`tcg-expedition`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Expedition Base Set opened the e-Card era.`,stat:`EX`,sub:`2002. Do not confuse the name with Pokémon-EX.`,why:`Expedition is a Wizards set name. Pokémon-EX is a later card type, and the collision in speech is constant. Expedition cards have e-Reader stripes and a 2002 layout; a Pokémon-EX has a large EX in the name and a two-prize rule.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`ecard`,`ex-era`]},{id:`tcg-aquapolis`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Aquapolis is the middle e-Card set with Crystal types.`,stat:`AQ`,sub:`2003. e-Reader. Crystal Pokémon begin here.`,why:`Aquapolis sits between Expedition and Skyridge. Crystal-type cards appear, and the e-Reader strip stays. It had First Edition and Unlimited with the messy late-Wizards timing. File it as e-Card, not as Aqua from later water sets, and not as Team Aqua SP cards.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`ecard`]},{id:`tcg-neo-destiny-shiny`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Neo Destiny printed Dark and Light Pokémon plus more Shinings.`,stat:`N4`,sub:`Last Neo set. Shining legends live here and in Revelation.`,why:`Neo Destiny is a 2001–2002 Wizards set with Dark and Light dual-look Pokémon and Shining secrets. Light and Dark here are Neo names, not Sword & Shield Darkness Ablaze and not Destined Rivals Team Rocket's. Era words get reused. Set symbols do not.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`neo`]},{id:`tcg-jungle-no-symbol-error`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Some early Jungle cards exist with a missing set symbol.`,stat:`ERR`,sub:`That is a known print variant, still a Jungle card.`,why:`Wizards Jungle is supposed to show a flower symbol. Copies without the symbol are documented factory variants. They are Jungle cards with a missing ink or plate moment, not a secret fourth Base Set print. Error plus set plus condition is how you describe them; not a 1-in-N.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`jungle`,`error`]},{id:`tcg-pocket-not-paper`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Pokémon TCG Pocket is a phone game with its own packs.`,stat:`2024`,sub:`Digital drop tables are not English paper tables.`,why:`Pokémon TCG Pocket launched in 2024 as a mobile TCG. Its god packs, hourglasses, and wonder picks are Pocket rules. They do not describe a Surging Sparks booster from a grocery peg. When a friend quotes a Pocket rate, ask which game.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pocket`,`pack`]},{id:`tcg-live-not-paper`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`TCG Live pack odds are a digital table.`,stat:`LIVE`,sub:`The code card opens Live. It does not copy your paper hits.`,why:`Redeeming a code gives a digital pack in Pokémon TCG Live with Live's drop rates. Pulling Moonbreon on paper does not place Moonbreon on your Live account, and the reverse is also false. Two economies. Two tables; one hobby nickname.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`live`,`pack`]},{id:`tcg-prerelease-stamp`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Prerelease stamps mark early event promos.`,stat:`PRE`,sub:`Often a staff-stamped or printed stamp on a holo.`,why:`Prerelease weekends stamp or print a unique mark on a promo from the upcoming set. That card is a promo printing. It may not be Standard-legal the day you open it. Collectors hunt centered stamps; players wait for the two-week legality timer.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`promo`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-build-battle`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A Build and Battle box is a prerelease kit, not an Elite Trainer Box.`,stat:`B&B`,sub:`Ready-to-play 40 plus a few packs of the new set.`,why:`Build & Battle boxes exist so shops can run prereleases. They include a 40-card deck with specific stamped or exclusive cards plus booster packs. Pack count is not nine, and it is not 36. Do not file a sealed B&B as an ETB on a heat board.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-theme-deck`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Theme decks and ex battle decks are constructed products, not boosters.`,stat:`DECK`,sub:`Fixed lists. No SIR slot hiding in the 60.`,why:`A theme deck, league battle deck, or ex battle deck contains a known 60 plus a playmat or accessories. You can read the list. Randomized illustration rares live in boosters, not in the fixed deck. Opening a battle deck looking for a special illustration rare is the wrong SKU.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-collection-box`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A collection box is a small sealed SKU with a few packs and a promo.`,stat:`COLL`,sub:`Pack count is on the back. Often three to six.`,why:`Poster collections, illustration collections, and tin collections bundle a handful of packs with a promo or an oversized card. They are not Elite Trainer Boxes and not booster boxes. Always read the pack count. Never inherit nine from the ETB sitting next to it.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-tin-packs`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Tins usually hide a short stack of packs behind a figure or promo.`,stat:`TIN`,sub:`Set mix inside a tin can be current or leftover.`,why:`TPC tins often include three or four packs that may match the tin's theme set or may be a mixed current assortment, depending on the SKU. Read the fine print. A Charizard tin does not guarantee Obsidian Flames, 151, or Darkness Ablaze. It guarantees whatever the panel lists.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`charizard`]},{id:`tcg-blister`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`A blister is a peg-pack: one or three packs plus a coin or promo.`,stat:`BLISTER`,sub:`Checklane blisters sit at registers. Same pack table inside.`,why:`Single-pack and three-pack blisters are how grocery and big-box stores hang boosters. The pack inside is a normal pack of that set. The extra coin or promo is the blister's add-on. You are not buying a secret better table; you are buying convenient plastic.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`pack`]},{id:`tcg-case-24`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`An English booster case is typically six boxes, 216 packs.`,stat:`CASE`,sub:`A case is logistics. It is not a printed hit guarantee.`,why:`Distributors ship English booster boxes in cases of six, which is 216 packs. Cases can still miss every chase. Case talk is volume, not a factory promise of one Moonbreon per case.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`pack`]},{id:`tcg-jp-sr-guarantee`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Many Japanese boxes guarantee a super rare or better.`,stat:`SR+`,sub:`English 36-pack boxes do not print that promise.`,why:`Japanese main boxes often include a hit guarantee on the insert, which is one reason people compare Japanese sealed to English sealed. English 36-pack boxes randomize without that printed floor. Translate the product before you translate the odds.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`japanese`,`english`,`sealed`]},{id:`tcg-energy-types-eight`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Modern Basic Energy is eight types.`,stat:`8`,sub:`Grass, Fire, Water, Lightning, Psychic, Fighting, Darkness, Metal.`,why:`Fairy Basic Energy left with Sword & Shield. Dragon never had a Basic Energy; Dragon attacks use colorless or specials. Colorless Pokémon are a type line, not a ninth Basic. The eight basics in the back of an SV pack are the whole basic rainbow.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`energy`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-dragon-no-basic`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Dragon-type Pokémon have no Basic Dragon Energy.`,stat:`DRAGON`,sub:`They eat colorless costs or special energy.`,why:`Dragon is a TCG type without a matching Basic Energy. Rayquaza lines often want a mix of typed energy plus colorless. That is why Double Colorless and other specials show up in Dragon decks. Type, energy, and pack energy are three lists.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`energy`,`play`,`rayquaza`]},{id:`tcg-darkness-metal-neo`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Darkness and Metal entered as types in Neo Genesis, not in Team Rocket.`,stat:`2000`,sub:`Team Rocket's Dark names were still Fire, Grass, Water.`,why:`Neo Genesis printed Darkness Energy and Metal Energy as basics and rettyped some Pokémon. Team Rocket's Dark Charizard the year before was a Fire Pokémon with Dark in the name. Destined Rivals' Team Rocket's Mewtwo is a 2025 owner card. Three uses of the word dark.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`energy`,`neo`,`team-rocket`]},{id:`tcg-baby-pokemon`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Baby Pokémon in Neo are Basics that can evolve into other Basics.`,stat:`BABY`,sub:`Pichu, Cleffa, Igglybuff. Extra deck steps.`,why:`Neo printed Baby Pokémon as their own stage with attacks that often flipped coins to become useless. They evolve into a Basic such as Pikachu. That extra step is why vintage Pichu lines look odd next to a modern Basic Pikachu. Stage text is the law.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`neo`,`play`,`pikachu`]},{id:`tcg-break-xy`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`BREAK cards in XY sit on top of a Pokémon and keep its attacks.`,stat:`BREAK`,sub:`Half-card look. Not a VMAX. Not a Mega.`,why:`XY BREAK Pokémon are a short extra stage with a landscape frame. They keep the underlying attacks and add one. They are not Mega Evolutions and not LV.X. When a 2016 stack looks like a card snapped in half, that is BREAK, not a miscut.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`xy`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-vunion`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`VUNION cards are four pieces that assemble in play.`,stat:`VUNION`,sub:`Sword and Shield. Four cards in the deck for one attacker.`,why:`A Pokémon VUNION is four cards that combine onto the bench. You must run all four pieces. They are a SWSH gimmick, not a LEGEND from HGSS, even though both use multiple pieces. Piece count is a deckbuilding cost; it is not a pull rate.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-delta-species`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Delta Species are EX-era Pokémon with an unexpected type.`,stat:`δ`,sub:`A Water Charizard is the teaching example.`,why:`Holon Phantom-era Delta Species print a δ in the name and a type the Pokédex does not use, such as a Metal or Water Charizard. Weakness follows the card. A Delta Charizard is an EX-era Holon card, not a Tera Charizard from Obsidian Flames. Crystal body versus Holon δ.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`ex-era`,`charizard`]},{id:`tcg-holon`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Holon is an EX-era setting, not a Scarlet and Violet location.`,stat:`HOLON`,sub:`Holon Energy and Delta Species live here.`,why:`EX Ruby & Sapphire's later sets wander into Holon, a place that lets Pokémon change type. Holon Energy cards are Special Energy from that story. Paldea, Kitakami, and Area Zero are SV geography. Do not file Holon next to Area Zero in a region binder.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`ex-era`]},{id:`tcg-unown`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Unown cards print a letter. The set still has a normal number.`,stat:`UNOWN`,sub:`Neo Discovery made them famous on paper.`,why:`Unown's Pokédex gimmick is the alphabet. TCG Unown cards show a letter form and still carry a set number like 1/75. The letter is flavor and sometimes an attack name. It is not a regulation mark; g on a 2023 card is a play letter, not Unown G.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`neo`,`play`]},{id:`tcg-owner-name-line`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`If the name line says Iono's Bellibolt, the owner is part of the card name.`,stat:`NAME`,sub:`It does not count as a regular Bellibolt for the four-copy rule.`,why:`Trainer's Pokémon include the trainer in the name, so N's Zoroark and a plain Zoroark are different cards. You can play four of each. Destined Rivals and Journey Together make this relevant again. Catalog by the full name line, not by the art of the Pokémon alone.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`journey-together`,`destined-rivals`]},{id:`tcg-iono`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Iono is a Supporter that shuffles both hands and draws by remaining prizes.`,stat:`IONO`,sub:`Paldea Evolved made the card. Journey Together made Iono's Pokémon.`,why:`Iono the trainer card is a disruption Supporter from Paldea Evolved. Iono's Bellibolt and friends in Journey Together are owner Pokémon, different cards. Same character, two set jobs. Play Iono as a Supporter; collect Iono's Pokémon as a name-line chase.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`paldea-evolved`,`journey-together`]},{id:`tcg-ogerpon`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Ogerpon's four masks are four TCG types in Twilight Masquerade.`,stat:`MASK`,sub:`Teal, Wellspring, Hearthflame, Cornerstone.`,why:`Twilight Masquerade prints Ogerpon with Kitakami's four masks as different types and different cards. Teal Mask Ogerpon ex is the grass mascot. The special illustration rares of those masks are different numbers. One legendary, four name lines, four binder slots.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`twilight-masquerade`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-terapagos`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Terapagos is the Stellar Crown legendary, not a Pikachu.`,stat:`SCR`,sub:`Area Zero turtle. Stellar Tera look.`,why:`Terapagos headlines Stellar Crown as a Tera Pokémon ex and as higher rarities. Surging Sparks later keeps Paldea's electric mascot energy with Pikachu ex. Two adjacent main sets, two different chases. Stellar is a Tera form word here, not a grade.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`stellar-crown`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-pecharunt`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Pecharunt and the Loyal Three are the Shrouded Fable villains.`,stat:`SFA`,sub:`Munkidori, Okidogi, Fezandipiti, then Kingambit as the closer.`,why:`Shrouded Fable is a short special set because the Kitakami myth is a short story on paper. Pecharunt ex and the Loyal Three sit next to toxic-style trainers. Mini-set still uses SV pack anatomy. It does not use a 36-pack English box.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`shrouded-fable`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-koraidon-miraidon`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Koraidon and Miraidon are the Scarlet and Violet box legendaries.`,stat:`SVI`,sub:`Past paradox bike and future paradox bike.`,why:`Scarlet's Koraidon and Violet's Miraidon open the English SV base set as ex cards and as later illustration treatments. Paradox Rift and Temporal Forces then fill in the rest of the paradox roster. The box legendary is a mascot. It is not automatically the thinnest SIR.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sv`,`paradox-rift`]},{id:`tcg-walking-wake`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Walking Wake is the ancient paradox Suicune in Temporal Forces.`,stat:`ANCIENT`,sub:`Iron Leaves is the future paradox Virizion on the other half.`,why:`Temporal Forces split its marketing down the paradox line: Walking Wake with Ancient cards, Iron Leaves with Future cards. They have playable ex versions and painted secrets. Ancient/Future is the mechanic. Suicune/Virizion is the Pokédex joke.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`temporal-forces`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-roaring-moon`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Roaring Moon is Paradox Rift's ancient paradox Salamence.`,stat:`PAR`,sub:`Iron Valiant is the future paradox on the other poster.`,why:`Paradox Rift's box duo is Roaring Moon and Iron Valiant. They are paradox Pokémon, not Mega Evolutions and not VMAX. Later Temporal Forces added the Ancient and Future rule boxes those teams wanted. Species nickname is not the era frame.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`paradox-rift`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-bloodmoon`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Bloodmoon Ursaluna is a Twilight Masquerade Kitakami card.`,stat:`TWM`,sub:`A separate Ursaluna, not the Hisui Ursaluna from Astral Radiance.`,why:`Bloodmoon Ursaluna ex is a Kitakami form printed in Twilight Masquerade. Hisuian Ursaluna from Astral Radiance is a Sword & Shield card. Same evolutionary family, different region, different era, different regulation mark. Form names exist so you do not sleeve the wrong one.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`twilight-masquerade`,`swsh`]},{id:`tcg-n-pokemon`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`N's Pokémon in Journey Together use N's name on the line.`,stat:`N`,sub:`Zoroark is the poster. Unova's N, not a regulation N.`,why:`Journey Together prints N's Zorua and N's Zoroark as trainer's Pokémon. The N is the Unova champion character from Black & White. It is not regulation mark N, which does not exist on the current letter list. Characters and letters share an alphabet; cards do not.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`journey-together`,`unova`]},{id:`tcg-cynthia-garchomp`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Cynthia's Garchomp in Destined Rivals is a 2025 owner card.`,stat:`DRI`,sub:`Not Platinum SP Garchomp, not a 2000s LV.X.`,why:`Destined Rivals reprints the idea of Cynthia's Garchomp as a modern trainer's Pokémon ex and as painted secrets. Platinum already did a Cynthia SP Garchomp in 2009. Same trainer, same dragon, new frame, new letter. Ask which cardboard before you compare chases.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`destined-rivals`,`platinum`]},{id:`tcg-ethans-hooh`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Ethan's Ho-Oh is a Destined Rivals Johto owner card.`,stat:`DRI`,sub:`Ethan is Gold's English trainer name.`,why:`Destined Rivals prints Ethan's Ho-Oh as a trainer's Pokémon, pairing a Johto protagonist with the Johto legendary. Gym Heroes once printed owner's Pokémon for Kanto leaders. The 2025 card is not a Neo Genesis Ho-Oh and not a Celebrations reprint. Owner plus set is the ID.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`destined-rivals`,`johto`]},{id:`tcg-team-rockets-mewtwo`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Team Rocket's Mewtwo is a Destined Rivals card, not Base Set Mewtwo.`,stat:`DRI`,sub:`Owner line. 2025 frame. Different number, different letter.`,why:`Base Set Mewtwo is 10/102, Wizards, 1999. Team Rocket's Mewtwo in Destined Rivals is a trainer's Pokémon from 2025 with Rocket in the name line. First movie Mewtwo promos are a third pile. The species is the easy part; the printing is the hobby.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`destined-rivals`,`mewtwo`,`base-set`]},{id:`tcg-zekrom-reshiram`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Zekrom and Reshiram split Black Bolt and White Flare.`,stat:`UNOVA`,sub:`Black Bolt is Zekrom. White Flare is Reshiram.`,why:`The dual Unova special sets split the box legendaries the way the games did. Illustration rares cover the Unova dex across both lists. Completing Black Bolt does not complete White Flare. Dual set means dual checklists, dual ETBs, dual hunt.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`black-bolt`,`white-flare`,`unova`]},{id:`tcg-unova-156`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Black Bolt and White Flare together aim at all 156 Unova Pokémon.`,stat:`156`,sub:`That is the dex goal, not the parallel-pattern goal.`,why:`Generation V added 156 Pokémon. The 2025 dual specials put each of them on paper, many with illustration rares. Poké Ball parallels are extra. If your goal is 'one of each Unova species,' you are not yet hunting Master Ball patterns; pick the binder goal first.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`black-bolt`,`white-flare`,`unova`]},{id:`tcg-sv-era-end`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Black Bolt and White Flare close the Scarlet and Violet era.`,stat:`SV END`,sub:`Mega Evolution starts the next block in September 2025.`,why:`Play and product both treat the Unova dual specials as the last Scarlet & Violet expansion wave. Mega Evolution on September 26, 2025 is a new series name on the wrapper. Cards from late SV can still be Standard-legal by letter after the era name changes.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sv`,`mega-evolution`,`black-bolt`]},{id:`tcg-standard-not-era`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Era names on the wrapper are product lines, not the Standard document.`,stat:`ERA`,sub:`A Mega Evolution card can be legal next to a Prismatic card.`,why:`Scarlet & Violet and Mega Evolution are branding on boosters. Standard asks for the regulation mark. H-mark Prismatic and I-mark Mega Evolution can share a 2026 Standard deck. Do not purge Prismatic because the new packs say Mega Evolution.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`rotation`,`mega-evolution`,`prismatic`]},{id:`tcg-prize-tax-summary`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Prize tax is how the rules charge you for a big attacker.`,stat:`1/2/3`,sub:`Basic one, ex two, Mega ex three, some Tag Teams three.`,why:`Knocking out a basic or Stage Pokémon takes one prize. Pokémon-EX, GX, V, and ex take two. Tag Team GX and Mega Evolution ex take three. That table is play, not pull; a three-prize Mega can still be a common double rare in the pack.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`mega-evolution`,`ex`]},{id:`tcg-reverse-patterns-change`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Reverse holo patterns change by era on purpose.`,stat:`REV`,sub:`Sun and Moon put an energy watermark in the body.`,why:`Wizards Legendary Collection fire reverses, EX-era reverses, BW plasma-looking foils, SM energy watermarks, and SV body foils are different recipes. A reverse is still 'the parallel of this number.' The pattern helps you date a card across the table without the set symbol.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`sm`,`vintage`]},{id:`tcg-full-art-word`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Full art is a layout, not a Scarlet and Violet rarity name.`,stat:`FA`,sub:`Ultra rares are the SV full-art ex. IRs are paintings.`,why:`People say full art for any edge-to-edge card. In SV vocabulary the textured full-art ex is ultra rare, the painted scene is illustration rare or special illustration rare, and the gold card is hyper rare. Using one nickname for all three hides the ladder you are hunting.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`sv`,`ur`,`ir`]},{id:`tcg-secret-rare-word`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Secret rare means the number ran past the set size.`,stat:`SECRET`,sub:`It does not mean one in two thousand all by itself.`,why:`Illustration rares, special illustration rares, ultra rares, hyper rares, Amazing Rares, Gold Stars, and alt-art VMAX are all secrets in casual speech. Only some of those have sourced 1-in-N tables, and those tables disagree with each other. Secret is numbering. Odds are a slot.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`]},{id:`tcg-alt-art-word`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Alt art in Sword and Shield usually meant a painted V or VMAX secret.`,stat:`ALT`,sub:`Scarlet and Violet renamed that job illustration rare and SIR.`,why:`Evolving Skies Moonbreon is an alternate-art VMAX, SWSH vocabulary. 151 Charizard is a special illustration rare, SV vocabulary. Both are painted chases numbered past the set. The nickname changed with the era; the lesson stayed: painted secrets are not the double-rare playable.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`rarity`,`evolving-skies`,`151`,`swsh`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-god-pack-word`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`God pack is a hobby nickname for a pack stuffed with secrets.`,stat:`GOD`,sub:`Japanese high-class packs are famous for them. Not a TPC SKU.`,why:`Japanese 151 and some high-class products can put a full secret line in one wrapper. English 151's demigod pack is a related, narrower pattern. Prismatic Evolutions also has notorious all-hit packs in the hobby conversation. None of those words is an official rarity; they are pack patterns.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pack`,`151`,`prismatic`,`japanese`]},{id:`tcg-etb-promo`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The Elite Trainer Box promo is a fixed card, not a random SIR.`,stat:`PROMO`,sub:`You can read which promo is in the box before you buy.`,why:`ETB promos are printed for that SKU, often a stamped illustration-style card. They are why people buy a box besides the nine packs. They are not a substitute for the special illustration rare slot inside the packs. Promo plus packs is the product; promo is not a lottery.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`etb`,`promo`]},{id:`tcg-energy-cosmos`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Some products pack cosmos-holo Basic Energy as extras.`,stat:`COSMOS`,sub:`151 boosters can include cosmos energy. Still Basic Energy.`,why:`Cosmos holo energy is a foil recipe on a Basic Energy, often from special sets or collections. It is pretty bulk. It is not ACE SPEC Energy and not a hyper rare. If the name line still says Basic Lightning Energy, the four-copy rule does not apply and the play type is Basic.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`energy`,`151`]},{id:`tcg-no-vintage-rate`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Catch'em does not publish 1-in-N tables for Wizards packs.`,stat:`NONE`,sub:`No Authentication Center sample sits on Base Set.`,why:`Moonbreon and 151 Charizard have counted modern tables. Base Set, Jungle, and Skyridge do not get a fake modern slot number. Holo rates from 1999 are a different manufacturing story, and we will not invent them. Vintage hunt talk stays on prints: 1st, Shadowless, Unlimited.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`vintage`,`base-set`,`pack`]},{id:`tcg-psa-10-modern-vs-vintage`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Modern cards 10 more often because the stock and print are cleaner.`,stat:`10`,sub:`A high modern pop 10 is not proof of a huge print run.`,why:`Wizards 1999 paper chips, prints off-center, and clouds in the holo. Scarlet & Violet stock is flatter and cleaner, so gem rates rise. That is why a 151 Charizard PSA 10 pop can dwarf a Base Set Charizard 10 pop without teaching you which pack was easier to find in stores.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`grade`,`vintage`,`151`,`charizard`]},{id:`tcg-binder-vs-deck`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Master set and Standard deck are different completion jobs.`,stat:`BINDER`,sub:`Reverses, IRs, and SIRs belong to the binder, not the 60.`,why:`A playable Paldea Evolved Iono is the Supporter. The Iono special illustration rare is the binder card with the same name and a different number. You may play either printing if the name and letter are legal. You cannot finish a master set with only the play stamp.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`paldea-evolved`]},{id:`tcg-number-not-dex`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Card numbers are binder indexes. National Dex numbers stay in the games.`,stat:`4≠006`,sub:`Base Charizard is 4/102. The Pokédex still says 006.`,why:`Every era reprints this confusion. 151 as a set name even jokes about it. When a listing says Charizard 199, that is the 151 secret number, not National Dex 199 which is not Charizard. Read both numbers; then read the set symbol.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`charizard`,`151`,`base-set`],cardId:`sv3pt5-199`},{id:`tcg-swsh-etb-vs-sv`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Do not price a Sword and Shield ETB as if it held nine packs.`,stat:`8≠9`,sub:`Eight in SWSH. Nine in SV. Count, then divide.`,why:`Per-pack hunt math on an Elite Trainer Box starts with the pack count on that SKU. Sword & Shield regular ETBs are eight. Scarlet & Violet regular ETBs are nine. Using the wrong denominator quietly invents a pull rate; count packs first.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`etb`,`swsh`,`sv`]},{id:`tcg-english-box-not-universal`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Not every English set has a thirty-six-pack booster box.`,stat:`NO BB`,sub:`Specials skip it. Mains usually have it.`,why:`Evolving Skies, Obsidian Flames, Surging Sparks, Destined Rivals, Journey Together, Paldea Evolved, Temporal Forces, Twilight Masquerade, Stellar Crown, and Paradox Rift have English boxes. 151, Prismatic Evolutions, Paldean Fates, Shrouded Fable, Crown Zenith, and Black Bolt / White Flare do not. Look up the SKU.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`sealed`,`151`,`prismatic`,`destined-rivals`]},{id:`tcg-mew-sir-not-ancient`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The 151 Mew special illustration rare is a 2023 card.`,stat:`151`,sub:`Ancient Mew is a 2000 movie promo with different script.`,why:`151's painted Mew is a Scarlet & Violet secret with a set number past 165. Ancient Mew is hieroglyphic movie cardboard from The Power of One. Neither is Base Set, which did not print Mew at all: Mew was the promo monster then, and it is the set name now.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`151`,`mew`,`promo`,`vintage`]},{id:`tcg-pikachu-many`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Pikachu is a species, not a set, not a rarity, not a grade.`,stat:`025`,sub:`Base 58/102, Amazing Rare, Surging Sparks SIR, McDonald's, 30th.`,why:`Pikachu has lived in every era's product line, so the card you mean has a set symbol and a number. Surging Sparks Pikachu ex SIR is a 2024 painted secret, Base Set Pikachu is 58/102, and 30th Celebration's Pikachu rares are a 2026 anniversary gimmick. Ask which printing.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`pikachu`,`surging-sparks`,`base-set`],cardId:`base1-58`},{id:`tcg-umbreon-many`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Umbreon chases live in Neo, Evolving Skies, and Prismatic.`,stat:`197`,sub:`Moonbreon is a VMAX alt. Sunbreon is an ex SIR.`,why:`Neo Genesis printed the first English Umbreon. Evolving Skies printed Moonbreon, the alt-art VMAX, at about 1 in 2,000, and Prismatic Evolutions printed Sunbreon, the ex SIR, at about 1 in 1,440. Three eras and three frames share one Pokédex number, so use the nickname, then confirm the set.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`umbreon`,`evolving-skies`,`prismatic`,`neo`],cardId:`swsh7-215`},{id:`tcg-charizard-many`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Charizard is the hobby's longest identity crisis.`,stat:`006`,sub:`Base 4/102, Evolutions, Champion's Path, 151, Obsidian Tera.`,why:`Wizards 4/102, XY Evolutions, Burning Shadows GX, Champion's Path V, Darkness Ablaze VMAX, 151 SIR, Obsidian Flames Tera SIR, and more all answer to Charizard. Pull rates and prints do not transfer. The 151 SIR is 1 in 225. The Obsidian SIR is 1 in 192; Moonbreon is not in this sentence.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`charizard`,`151`,`obsidian-flames`,`base-set`],cardId:`base1-4`},{id:`tcg-greninja-twilight`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The Greninja everyone means in 2024 is a Twilight Masquerade card.`,stat:`TWM`,sub:`Special illustration rare. H-mark set. Main 36-pack box.`,why:`Twilight Masquerade's Greninja ex SIR is a later-SV painted chase in a set whose any-SIR slot sat near one in 86 packs. XY and the anime also made Greninja famous, and those older cards still exist. If the conversation is 2024 paper, start with TWM, then confirm the number.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`greninja`,`twilight-masquerade`]},{id:`tcg-lugia-tempest`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Silver Tempest Lugia V alternate art is a late Sword and Shield chase.`,stat:`SIT`,sub:`Thicker than Moonbreon in TCGPlayer's sample, still a secret.`,why:`TCGPlayer counted Lugia V alt near one in 674 English Silver Tempest packs. Crown Zenith later printed more Lugia gallery cards in a special set. Two Lugia products at the end of Galar means two SKUs and two lists, and the alt V is the Tempest pack chase.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`swsh`],cardId:`swsh12-186`},{id:`tcg-giratina-two`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Lost Origin Giratina V alt and Crown Zenith gold Giratina are different cards.`,stat:`LOR/CRZ`,sub:`One is a main-set alt. One is a special-set gold VSTAR.`,why:`Lost Origin's Giratina V alternate art is a 2022 main-set secret with a counted pack rate. Crown Zenith's gold Giratina VSTAR is a 2023 special-set hyper-style gold. Both are Giratina. Both are Sword & Shield; neither is a Scarlet & Violet illustration rare.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`lost-origin`,`crown-zenith`,`giratina`],cardId:`swsh11-186`},{id:`tcg-eevee-nine`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Eevee plus eight evolutions is nine Pokémon on Prismatic's poster.`,stat:`9`,sub:`Sylveon closed the line in XY. Prismatic put them all on SIRs.`,why:`Vaporeon, Jolteon, Flareon, Espeon, Umbreon, Leafeon, Glaceon, and Sylveon are the eight. Prismatic Evolutions built a special set around that family, Evolving Skies did the Sword & Shield version with V and VMAX, and Neo did Espeon and Umbreon first. The family is the theme; the set is the product.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`eevee`,`prismatic`,`evolving-skies`]},{id:`tcg-rotation-letter-location`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`The regulation mark sits near the bottom with the artist credit.`,stat:`LETTER`,sub:`It is not the set symbol and not the rarity icon.`,why:`Set symbol names the expansion, rarity icon names the rank, and regulation letter names the Standard year. All three can sit on one card and disagree with casual speech. G-mark 151 is out of 2026 Standard, but a Mega Evolution I-mark reprint of a trainer from 151's year can still play.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`rotation`]},{id:`tcg-expanded-not-unlimited`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Unlimited format is the vintage kitchen-table pool, not Expanded.`,stat:`UNL`,sub:`Expanded starts much later than Wizards Base Set.`,why:`Play! Pokémon Expanded is a long modern-ish pool with a ban list. Unlimited, in casual speech, means every card ever, Wizards included. Those are not the same tournament document. Rotation articles about G leaving Standard do not write the Unlimited house rules at your kitchen table.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`vintage`]},{id:`tcg-play-pokemon`,kicker:`TCG`,headline:`Play! Pokémon is the organized-play body that publishes rotation.`,stat:`PLAY`,sub:`pokemon.com announcements, not a Discord rumor.`,why:`Standard dates, regulation letters, and the two-week legality wait come from Play! Pokémon. Catch'em's rotation teaching follows those posts. A shophouse rule can be stricter. A YouTube thumbnail cannot rotate a letter; when G left, it left because Play! Pokémon said so on January 9, 2026.`,tags:[`tcg`,`fact`,`play`,`rotation`]}],i={note:e,asOf:t,count:296,facts:r};export{t as asOf,n as count,i as default,r as facts,e as note};